ForgeData.register("gcse-maths",{"GCSE-MATH-P1":{"label":"Paper 1 — Non-Calculator","color":"#1d4ed8","questions":[{"id":"MATH-P1-01","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Write 0.00072 in standard form.","options":{"A":"7.2 × 10⁻⁴","B":"72 × 10⁻⁴","C":"72 × 10⁻⁵","D":"0.72 × 10⁻³"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-NUMBER","scaffold":"Move the decimal four places right to make 7.2, so the power is −4: 0.00072 = 7.2 × 10⁻⁴.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is equal to 3.6 × 10⁵?","options":{"A":"36,000","B":"3,600,000","C":"360,000","D":"0.00036"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-02","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"What is the highest common factor of 36 and 48?","options":{"A":"6","B":"12","C":"8","D":"18"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-FACTORS","scaffold":"The common factors include 1, 2, 3, 4, 6 and 12; the greatest is 12.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the lowest common multiple of 6 and 8?","options":{"A":"12","B":"18","C":"48","D":"24"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-03","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Simplify 3x + 5 − x + 2.","options":{"A":"2x + 3","B":"4x + 7","C":"2x + 7","D":"4x + 3"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-ALGEBRA","scaffold":"Collect like terms: 3x − x = 2x and 5 + 2 = 7, giving 2x + 7.","reforge":{"stem":"Expand 4(2x − 3).","options":{"A":"8x − 12","B":"8x − 3","C":"6x − 12","D":"8x + 12"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-04","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Solve 5x − 7 = 18.","options":{"A":"x = 2","B":"x = 25","C":"x = 7/5","D":"x = 5"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-EQUATIONS","scaffold":"Add 7 to both sides to get 5x = 25, then divide by 5: x = 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 3y + 4 = 19.","options":{"A":"y = 15","B":"y = 5","C":"y = 23/3","D":"y = 7"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-05","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A ratio is 3:5 and the total is  forty. What is the larger share?","options":{"A":"25","B":"20","C":"15","D":"30"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-RATIO","scaffold":"There are 8 parts, so each part is 40 ÷ 8 = 5. The larger share is 5 × 5 = 25.","reforge":{"stem":"Divide £56 in the ratio 3:4. What is the larger amount?","options":{"A":"£24","B":"£28","C":"£32","D":"£35"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-06","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Increase 80 by 15%.","options":{"A":"£94","B":"£92","C":"£95","D":"£96"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PERCENT","scaffold":"15% of 80 is 12, so the increased value is 80 + 12 = 92.","reforge":{"stem":"A value of 60 is decreased by 25%. What is the result?","options":{"A":"35","B":"40","C":"48","D":"45"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-07","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"The angles in a triangle are x, 2x and 3x. Find x.","options":{"A":"20°","B":"45°","C":"30°","D":"60°"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-ANGLES","scaffold":"Angles in a triangle total 180°, so 6x = 180 and x = 30°.","reforge":{"stem":"An isosceles triangle has equal angles of 50°. What is the third angle?","options":{"A":"70°","B":"50°","C":"80°","D":"100°"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-08","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"What is the area of a rectangle measuring 7 cm by 4 cm?","options":{"A":"11 cm²","B":"22 cm²","C":"44 cm²","D":"28 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-MEASURES","scaffold":"Area of a rectangle = length × width = 7 × 4 = 28 cm².","reforge":{"stem":"A parallelogram has base 9 cm and perpendicular height 4 cm. Find its area.","options":{"A":"13 cm²","B":"36 cm²","C":"18 cm²","D":"72 cm²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-09","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A fair six-sided die is rolled. What is the probability of an even number?","options":{"A":"1/2","B":"0.37","C":"1/6","D":"1.37"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY","scaffold":"The even outcomes are 2, 4 and 6: 3 favourable outcomes out of 6, which simplifies to 1/2.","reforge":{"stem":"A bag contains 3 red and 2 blue counters. What is P(blue)?","options":{"A":"2.70","B":"100","C":"2/5","D":"1/2"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-10","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Find the mean of 4, 6, 9 and 11.","options":{"A":"6.0","B":"7.5","C":"7.0","D":"8.0"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-STATISTICS","scaffold":"Add the values to get 30, then divide by 4: mean = 7.5.","reforge":{"stem":"The mean of five numbers is 8. What is their total?","options":{"A":"13","B":"32","C":"45","D":"40"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-11","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"The sequence 5, 9, 13, 17 continues with which term?","options":{"A":"20","B":"22","C":"21","D":"25"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-NTH","scaffold":"The common difference is 4, so the next term is 17 + 4 = 21.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the nth term of 4, 7, 10, 13, …?","options":{"A":"3n + 1","B":"4n − 1","C":"n + 3","D":"3n − 1"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-12","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","options":{"A":"(x + 2)(x + 6)","B":"(x + 1)(x + 12)","C":"(x − 3)(x − 4)","D":"(x + 3)(x + 4)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-FACTORISE","scaffold":"Find two numbers that multiply to 12 and add to 7: 3 and 4. Therefore x² + 7x + 12 = (x + 3)(x + 4).","reforge":{"stem":"Factorise x² − 9.","options":{"A":"(x − 9)(x + 1)","B":"(x − 3)(x + 3)","C":"(x − 3)²","D":"(x + 9)(x − 1)"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-13","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Simplify 3/4 + 5/8.","options":{"A":"1 3/8","B":"11/8","C":"1 1/8","D":"8/12"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-FRACTIONS","scaffold":"Use eighths: 3/4 = 6/8, so 6/8 + 5/8 = 11/8 = 1 3/8.","reforge":{"stem":"Calculate 5/6 − 1/4.","options":{"A":"1/2","B":"2/10","C":"7/12","D":"4/12"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-14","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Simplify √50.","options":{"A":"25√2","B":"5√2","C":"√25","D":"10√5"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-SURDS","scaffold":"√50 = √(25 × 2) = 5√2.","reforge":{"stem":"Simplify 3√8.","options":{"A":"3√2","B":"24","C":"8√3","D":"6√2"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-15","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"If a = 3 and b = −2, find 2a² + b.","options":{"A":"10","B":"14","C":"16","D":"22"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SUBSTITUTION","scaffold":"Substitute carefully: 2(3²) + (−2) = 18 − 2 = 16.","reforge":{"stem":"If p = −4 and q = 2, find p² − 3q.","options":{"A":"10","B":"14","C":"22","D":"28"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-16","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"The nth term of a sequence is 5n − 2. Find the 10th term.","options":{"A":"58","B":"n = 23","C":"n = 4","D":"48"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-NTH","scaffold":"Substitute n = 10: 5(10) − 2 = 48.","reforge":{"stem":"The nth term is 3n + 4. Which term is 34?","options":{"A":"n = 6","B":"n = 10","C":"n = 9","D":"n = 12"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-17","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Two parallel lines are crossed by a transversal. One corresponding angle is 68°. Find the matching angle.","options":{"A":"68°","B":"22°","C":"112°","D":"292°"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PARALLEL","scaffold":"Corresponding angles are equal when parallel lines are crossed by a transversal, so the matching angle is 68°.","reforge":{"stem":"An angle on a straight line is 137°. Find the adjacent angle.","options":{"A":"137°","B":"53°","C":"43°","D":"223°"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-18","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A sector has radius 6 cm and angle 60°. Find its area in terms of π.","options":{"A":"12π cm²","B":"6π cm²","C":"18π cm²","D":"36π cm²"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-SECTOR","scaffold":"Sector area = 60/360 × π × 6² = 6π cm².","reforge":{"stem":"Find the arc length of a semicircle with radius 5 cm in terms of π.","options":{"A":"50π cm","B":"10π cm","C":"25π cm","D":"5π cm"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-19","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A spinner has 4 equal sections. What is the probability of landing on red if 3 sections are red?","options":{"A":"1/4","B":"1/2","C":"3/4","D":"1"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SIMPLEPROB","scaffold":"Three of the four equally likely sections are red, so the probability is 3/4.","reforge":{"stem":"A fair coin is tossed twice. What is P(two heads)?","options":{"A":"1/4","B":"1/2","C":"2/3","D":"3/4"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-20","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"What is the median of 3, 8, 4, 10 and 6?","options":{"A":"The mean must equal 18","B":"−2","C":"18 is the most frequently occurring value","D":"6"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-MEDIAN","scaffold":"Order the values 3, 4, 6, 8, 10. The middle value is 6.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the mode of 2, 4, 4, 5, 7, 4?","options":{"A":"The range is 18","B":"4","C":"5","D":"7"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-21","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Evaluate 2³ × 2⁴.","options":{"A":"2⁷","B":"4⁷","C":"2¹²","D":"8⁴"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-INDICES","scaffold":"Add indices for the same base: 2³ × 2⁴ = 2⁷.","reforge":{"stem":"Simplify (3x²)(2x³).","options":{"A":"6x⁶","B":"5x⁵","C":"6x⁵","D":"9x⁵"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-22","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Write 0.375 as a fraction in simplest form.","options":{"A":"5/12","B":"3/8","C":"7/16","D":"3/5"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-FRACTIONS2","scaffold":"0.375 = 375/1000 = 3/8.","reforge":{"stem":"Which fraction equals 0.625?","options":{"A":"2/3","B":"3/5","C":"7/10","D":"5/8"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-23","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A map has scale 1:50 000. What map distance represents 2 km?","options":{"A":"40 cm","B":"25 cm","C":"4 cm","D":"100 cm"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SCALE","scaffold":"2 km = 200 000 cm; divide by 50 000 to get 4 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"A drawing uses scale 1:20. A length is 6 cm on it. Find the real length.","options":{"A":"120 cm","B":"30 cm","C":"26 cm","D":"0.3 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-24","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Solve 2(x + 3) = 18.","options":{"A":"x = 15","B":"x = 9","C":"x = 12","D":"x = 6"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-EQUATIONS","scaffold":"x + 3 = 9, so x = 6.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 4y − 5 = 2y + 9.","options":{"A":"y = 2","B":"y = 7","C":"y = 14","D":"y = −7"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-25","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Which inequality means x is at least 4?","options":{"A":"x ≥ 4","B":"x ≥ −3","C":"x ≤ 4","D":"x < 4"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"At least includes 4, so use ≥.","reforge":{"stem":"Which values satisfy x < −2?","options":{"A":"0 and 2","B":"−2 and −1","C":"−3 and −4","D":"−1 and 3"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-26","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Reflect (3, −2) in the y-axis.","options":{"A":"(3, 2)","B":"(−3, −2)","C":"7.2 × 10⁴","D":"(2, −3)"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-COORDINATES","scaffold":"A y-axis reflection changes the sign of x only.","reforge":{"stem":"Reflect (−4, 1) in the x-axis.","options":{"A":"72 × 10⁻⁵","B":"(4, 1)","C":"(4, −1)","D":"(−4, −1)"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-27","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A quadrilateral has angles 82°, 96°, 104° and x°. Find x.","options":{"A":"98°","B":"88°","C":"78°","D":"108°"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-ANGLES","scaffold":"360 − 82 − 96 − 104 = 78°.","reforge":{"stem":"An exterior triangle angle is 126°. One opposite interior angle is 58°. Find the other.","options":{"A":"68°","B":"74°","C":"84°","D":"116°"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-28","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Find the area of a triangle with base 12 cm and height 7 cm.","options":{"A":"38 cm²","B":"84 cm²","C":"19 cm²","D":"42 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-AREA2","scaffold":"Area = 1/2 × 12 × 7 = 42 cm².","reforge":{"stem":"A trapezium has parallel sides 8 cm and 14 cm and height 5 cm. Find its area.","options":{"A":"70 cm²","B":"55 cm²","C":"110 cm²","D":"35 cm²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-29","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A cuboid measures 3 cm by 4 cm by 5 cm. Find its volume.","options":{"A":"60 cm³","B":"47 cm³","C":"12 cm³","D":"20 cm³"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-VOLUME","scaffold":"Volume = 3 × 4 × 5 = 60 cm³.","reforge":{"stem":"A prism has area 18 cm² and length 7 cm. Find its volume.","options":{"A":"63 cm³","B":"25 cm³","C":"126 cm³","D":"144 cm³"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-30","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A fair spinner has five equal sections numbered 1 to 5. Find P(number > 3).","options":{"A":"3/5","B":"2/5","C":"1/5","D":"4/5"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY4","scaffold":"The successful outcomes are 4 and 5, so P = 2/5.","reforge":{"stem":"A bag has 4 white and 6 black counters. Find P(white).","options":{"A":"4/6","B":"3/5","C":"1/4","D":"2/5"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-31","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Find the range of 7, 12, 4, 19 and 9.","options":{"A":"19","B":"12","C":"15","D":"23"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-RANGE","scaffold":"Range = 19 − 4 = 15.","reforge":{"stem":"Which average is most affected by an extreme outlier?","options":{"A":"Mean","B":"Median","C":"Mode","D":"Quartile"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-32","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"The nth term is 4n + 1. Find the first term.","options":{"A":"0","B":"4","C":"1","D":"5"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-NTH","scaffold":"Set n = 1: 4(1) + 1 = 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the nth term of 7, 11, 15, 19, …","options":{"A":"3n + 4","B":"4n + 3","C":"7n − 3","D":"4n − 3"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-33","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Solve x² = 49.","options":{"A":"x = 7 or −7","B":"x = 7","C":"No real roots at all","D":"x = 49"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC2","scaffold":"Both 7 and −7 have square 49.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve x² − 9 = 0.","options":{"A":"x = −9","B":"x = 9","C":"x = 3 or −3","D":"Two distinct real roots"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-34","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"What is the reciprocal of 5/8?","options":{"A":"5/8","B":"8/5","C":"−5/8","D":"3/8"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-RECIPROCAL","scaffold":"Swap numerator and denominator: 8/5.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the reciprocal of 0.2?","options":{"A":"2","B":"0.5","C":"0.02","D":"5"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-35","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A regular hexagon has side length 6 cm. Find its perimeter.","options":{"A":"24 cm","B":"30 cm","C":"36 cm","D":"42 cm"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-POLYGON","scaffold":"Six equal sides give 6 × 6 = 36 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"A regular octagon has perimeter 56 cm. Find its side length.","options":{"A":"7 cm","B":"8 cm","C":"6 cm","D":"9 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-36","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A translation moves 4 right and 3 down. Which vector represents it?","options":{"A":"(−3, −4)","B":"(−4, 3)","C":"(3, 4)","D":"(4, −3)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TRANSFORM","scaffold":"Right is positive x and down is negative y: (4, −3).","reforge":{"stem":"What does vector (−2, 5) do to a point?","options":{"A":"2 right and 5 down","B":"2 left and 5 up","C":"5 left and 2 up","D":"5 right and 2 down"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-37","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A circle has diameter 10 cm. Find its radius.","options":{"A":"5 cm","B":"10 cm","C":"20 cm","D":"2.5 cm"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLE","scaffold":"Radius = diameter ÷ 2 = 5 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the circumference of a circle with radius r?","options":{"A":"πd²","B":"πr²","C":"2πr","D":"r²"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-38","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"A line has gradient 2 and passes through (0, 5). Find its equation.","options":{"A":"y = 5x + 2","B":"y = 2x + 5","C":"y = 2x − 5","D":"y = −2x + 5"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-LINEARGRAPH","scaffold":"Use y = mx + c with m = 2 and c = 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the gradient through (0, 3) and (2, 9).","options":{"A":"12","B":"6","C":"2","D":"3"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-39","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Convert 3.5 hours into minutes.","options":{"A":"350","B":"180","C":"210","D":"35"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-TIME","scaffold":"3.5 × 60 = 210 minutes.","reforge":{"stem":"Convert 2.4 kg into grams.","options":{"A":"2400 g","B":"240 g","C":"24 g","D":"24000 g"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-P1-40","spec":"1MA1 Paper 1","stem":"Estimate 49.8 × 2.1 by rounding to 1 significant figure.","options":{"A":"50","B":"80","C":"120","D":"100"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-ESTIMATION","scaffold":"50 × 2 = 100.","reforge":{"stem":"Estimate 398 ÷ 19 by rounding to 1 significant figure.","options":{"A":"10","B":"20","C":"40","D":"200"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-01","stem":"Work out 3/4 + 2/5.","options":{"A":"23/20","B":"5/9","C":"23/40","D":"5/20"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-FRACTIONS","scaffold":"Adding fractions needs a common denominator, never adding numerators and denominators separately. For 3/4 + 2/5 the lowest common denominator is 20: 3/4 = 15/20 and 2/5 = 8/20, so the sum is 23/20, or 1 3/20 as a mixed number. The answer 5/9 comes from adding tops and bottoms, which is the single most common error here — check by estimating: 3/4 is nearly 1 and 2/5 is nearly a half, so the answer must exceed 1.","reforge":{"stem":"Work out 2/3 × 3/8, giving your answer in its simplest form.","options":{"A":"5/11","B":"6/11","C":"1/4","D":"16/9"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-02","stem":"Work out 2/5 ÷ 3/10.","options":{"A":"6/50","B":"4/3","C":"3/4","D":"2/15"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-FRACTIONS2","scaffold":"To divide by a fraction, multiply by its reciprocal: 2/5 ÷ 3/10 = 2/5 × 10/3 = 20/15 = 4/3. Flipping the wrong fraction, or multiplying without flipping at all, produces the usual wrong answers. A sense check helps: 3/10 is smaller than 2/5, so the answer must be greater than 1, which rules out every option below 1 immediately.","reforge":{"stem":"Work out 1 1/2 ÷ 3/4.","options":{"A":"9/8","B":"4/9","C":"1 1/8","D":"2"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-03","stem":"Write 0.375 as a fraction in its simplest form.","options":{"A":"3/4","B":"375/100","C":"3/8","D":"1/3"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-DECIMALPERCENT","scaffold":"0.375 has three decimal places, so it equals 375/1000. Divide top and bottom by 125 to get 3/8. Useful conversions to memorise: 1/8 = 0.125, 1/4 = 0.25, 3/8 = 0.375, 1/2 = 0.5, 5/8 = 0.625, 3/4 = 0.75. A fraction gives a recurring decimal exactly when its simplified denominator has a prime factor other than 2 or 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Which fraction produces a recurring rather than a terminating decimal?","options":{"A":"4/15","B":"9/25","C":"5/16","D":"7/20"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-04","stem":"Express 0.4̇ (0.444...) as a fraction in its simplest form.","options":{"A":"1/9","B":"4/10","C":"44/100","D":"4/9"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-RECURRING","scaffold":"Let x = 0.444... Multiply by 10 because one digit recurs: 10x = 4.444... Subtracting gives 9x = 4, so x = 4/9. For two recurring digits multiply by 100 and divide by 99; for three, by 1000 and divide by 999. So 0.4̇5̇ = 45/99 = 5/11. Always simplify at the end and check by dividing back.","reforge":{"stem":"Express 0.2̇7̇ (0.272727...) as a fraction in its simplest form.","options":{"A":"27/100","B":"3/11","C":"27/90","D":"1/27"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-05","stem":"Simplify 5(2x − 3) − 2(x − 4).","options":{"A":"8x − 7","B":"8x − 23","C":"12x − 7","D":"8x + 5"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-ALGEBRA","scaffold":"Expand each bracket, watching the sign in front of the second: 5(2x − 3) = 10x − 15, and −2(x − 4) = −2x + 8. Note the minus times minus gives +8, which is where most errors occur. Collecting terms gives 10x − 2x = 8x and −15 + 8 = −7, so 8x − 7. Substituting a value such as x = 1 into both the original and your answer is a quick check.","reforge":{"stem":"Expand and simplify (x + 3)(x − 5).","options":{"A":"x² − 15","B":"x² + 2x − 15","C":"x² − 2x − 15","D":"x² − 2x + 15"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-06","stem":"Factorise fully 12x² + 18x.","options":{"A":"6(2x² + 3x)","B":"6x(2x + 3)","C":"3x(4x + 6)","D":"2x(6x + 9)"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-FACTORISE","scaffold":"Take out the highest common factor of both the numbers and the letters. The HCF of 12 and 18 is 6, and both terms contain x, so the factor is 6x, leaving 6x(2x + 3). The other options are all partially factorised: each still has a common factor inside the bracket, so none is 'fully' factorised. Expanding your answer is the fastest way to confirm it.","reforge":{"stem":"Factorise x² + 7x + 12.","options":{"A":"(x + 2)(x + 6)","B":"(x + 12)(x + 1)","C":"(x − 3)(x − 4)","D":"(x + 3)(x + 4)"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-07","stem":"Solve 4x − 7 = 2x + 9.","options":{"A":"x = 1","B":"x = 16","C":"x = 8","D":"x = 2"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-EQUATIONS","scaffold":"Collect the letters on one side and the numbers on the other, doing the same to both sides. Subtracting 2x gives 2x − 7 = 9; adding 7 gives 2x = 16; dividing by 2 gives x = 8. Substituting back is essential: 4(8) − 7 = 25 and 2(8) + 9 = 25, so both sides agree. Answer C is what you get by forgetting the final division.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 3(x + 2) = 21.","options":{"A":"x = 5","B":"x = 7","C":"x = 9","D":"x = 6.3"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-08","stem":"Solve the simultaneous equations 2x + y = 11 and x − y = 1.","options":{"A":"x = 3, y = 5","B":"x = 5, y = 4","C":"x = 6, y = 5","D":"x = 4, y = 3"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SIMULTANEOUS","scaffold":"Adding the two equations eliminates y because +y and −y cancel: 3x = 12, so x = 4. Substituting into x − y = 1 gives 4 − y = 1, so y = 3. Always substitute into the equation you did not use to derive x, and check both equations: 2(4) + 3 = 11 and 4 − 3 = 1. If the signs of the letter you want to remove match, subtract; if they differ, add.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 3x + 2y = 16 and x + 2y = 8.","options":{"A":"x = 2, y = 5","B":"x = 4, y = 2","C":"x = 3, y = 3.5","D":"x = 8, y = 0"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-09","stem":"The nth term of a sequence is 4n − 1. What is the 10th term?","options":{"A":"39","B":"40","C":"36","D":"41"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-NTH","scaffold":"Substitute n = 10 into 4n − 1: 4 × 10 = 40, then subtract 1 to get 39. To find the nth term of a linear sequence, the coefficient of n is the common difference, and the constant is the term that would come 'before the first', at n = 0. For 3, 7, 11, 15 the difference is 4 and the zeroth term is −1, giving 4n − 1 — the same sequence as here.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the nth term of the sequence 7, 12, 17, 22, ...","options":{"A":"7n + 5","B":"5n + 7","C":"5n + 2","D":"n + 5"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-10","stem":"A sequence begins 2, 6, 18, 54. What is the next term?","options":{"A":"108","B":"162","C":"58","D":"216"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-GEOMETRIC","scaffold":"Check for a common difference first; here there is none. Check for a common ratio: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, 18 ÷ 6 = 3, 54 ÷ 18 = 3, so the sequence is geometric with ratio 3 and the next term is 54 × 3 = 162. Answer A comes from doubling instead. Other sequence types to recognise: quadratic (second differences constant), triangular, square and Fibonacci-style.","reforge":{"stem":"Which sequence is quadratic?","options":{"A":"3, 6, 12, 24","B":"1, 1, 2, 3, 5","C":"5, 9, 13, 17","D":"2, 5, 10, 17"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-11","stem":"Simplify (x⁵ × x³) ÷ x².","options":{"A":"x⁷","B":"x¹⁰","C":"x⁶","D":"x⁴"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-INDICES","scaffold":"Multiplying powers of the same base adds the indices, dividing subtracts them: x⁵ × x³ = x⁸, then x⁸ ÷ x² = x⁶. Other rules: (xᵃ)ᵇ = x^(ab), x⁰ = 1, x⁻ⁿ = 1/xⁿ, and x^(1/2) = √x. Multiplying the indices instead of adding is the usual slip, and gives x¹⁵ ÷ x² here.","reforge":{"stem":"Evaluate 16^(3/4).","options":{"A":"8","B":"4","C":"64","D":"12"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-12","stem":"Simplify √50 + √8 in the form a√2.","options":{"A":"10√2","B":"√58","C":"5√2","D":"7√2"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SURDS","scaffold":"Simplify each surd by finding the largest square factor: √50 = √25 × √2 = 5√2, and √8 = √4 × √2 = 2√2. Since both are now multiples of √2 they can be added: 5√2 + 2√2 = 7√2. Surds can only be added when the number under the root matches, so √50 + √8 is not √58 — a check by decimal gives 7.07 + 2.83 ≈ 9.9, and 7√2 ≈ 9.9.","reforge":{"stem":"Rationalise the denominator of 6/√3.","options":{"A":"6√3","B":"2√3","C":"√2","D":"3√3"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-13","stem":"Increase 240 by 35%.","options":{"A":"324","B":"84","C":"275","D":"356"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PERCENT","scaffold":"Use a multiplier: increasing by 35% means multiplying by 1.35, so 240 × 1.35 = 324. Decreasing by 35% would use 0.65. Without a calculator, 10% of 240 is 24, so 30% is 72 and 5% is 12, giving 84 to add to 240 — again 324. Answer B is the increase itself rather than the new total, a distinction worth reading carefully in the question.","reforge":{"stem":"A price falls from 80 to 68. What is the percentage decrease?","options":{"A":"12%","B":"17.6%","C":"15%","D":"20%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-14","stem":"A bag contains red and blue counters in the ratio 3:7. There are 40 counters altogether. How many are blue?","options":{"A":"12","B":"28","C":"21","D":"30"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-RATIO","scaffold":"Add the ratio parts to find the total number of shares: 3 + 7 = 10. Divide the total by the number of shares: 40 ÷ 10 = 4 counters per share. Blue has 7 shares, so 7 × 4 = 28. Check the parts add back to the total: 12 red + 28 blue = 40. A frequent error is dividing by one of the ratio numbers rather than by their sum.","reforge":{"stem":"Two numbers are in the ratio 2:5. The larger is 35. What is the smaller?","options":{"A":"7","B":"10","C":"17.5","D":"14"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-15","stem":"Find the highest common factor of 24 and 60.","options":{"A":"6","B":"4","C":"12","D":"120"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-FACTORS","scaffold":"Write each number as a product of primes: 24 = 2³ × 3 and 60 = 2² × 3 × 5. The HCF takes the lowest power of each shared prime: 2² × 3 = 12. The LCM takes the highest power of every prime appearing: 2³ × 3 × 5 = 120, which is option D — so read carefully which is asked. A useful check is that HCF × LCM equals the product of the two numbers: 12 × 120 = 1440 = 24 × 60.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the lowest common multiple of 8 and 12.","options":{"A":"24","B":"4","C":"48","D":"96"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-16","stem":"Write 0.00046 in standard form.","options":{"A":"0.46 × 10⁻³","B":"46 × 10⁻⁵","C":"4.6 × 10⁴","D":"4.6 × 10⁻⁴"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-STANDARD2","scaffold":"Standard form is A × 10ⁿ where A is at least 1 and less than 10. Moving the point in 0.00046 four places right gives 4.6, and because the original number is smaller than 1 the index is negative: 4.6 × 10⁻⁴. Options B and D have coefficients outside the required range, so although they are numerically equal they are not in standard form — a distinction examiners test deliberately.","reforge":{"stem":"Work out (3 × 10⁵) × (2 × 10⁻²), giving your answer in standard form.","options":{"A":"6 × 10⁻¹⁰","B":"6 × 10³","C":"5 × 10³","D":"6 × 10⁷"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-17","stem":"A triangle has angles of 40° and 75°. What is the third angle?","options":{"A":"65°","B":"115°","C":"75°","D":"55°"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-ANGLES","scaffold":"Angles in a triangle sum to 180°, so the third is 180 − 40 − 75 = 65°. Related facts: angles on a straight line sum to 180°, angles around a point to 360°, and angles in a quadrilateral to 360°. In an isosceles triangle the two base angles are equal, which often supplies a missing value. Answer A is the sum of the two given angles rather than the remainder.","reforge":{"stem":"The exterior angle of a regular polygon is 30°. How many sides does it have?","options":{"A":"6","B":"10","C":"12","D":"30"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-18","stem":"What is the sum of the interior angles of a hexagon?","options":{"A":"1080°","B":"720°","C":"360°","D":"540°"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-POLYGON","scaffold":"Interior angle sum = (n − 2) × 180°, so for n = 6 that is 4 × 180 = 720°. For a regular polygon, divide by n to get each interior angle: 720 ÷ 6 = 120°. Exterior angles always sum to 360° regardless of the number of sides, so each exterior angle of a regular hexagon is 60°, and interior plus exterior always gives 180°.","reforge":{"stem":"Each interior angle of a regular polygon is 140°. How many sides does it have?","options":{"A":"7","B":"8","C":"10","D":"9"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-19","stem":"A right-angled triangle has shorter sides of 6 cm and 8 cm. How long is the hypotenuse?","options":{"A":"14 cm","B":"48 cm","C":"10 cm","D":"7 cm"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PYTHAGORAS","scaffold":"Pythagoras gives a² + b² = c², so 6² + 8² = 36 + 64 = 100, and c = √100 = 10 cm. To find a shorter side instead, subtract: c² − a² = b². Recognising the common triples 3-4-5, 5-12-13 and 8-15-17 (and multiples such as 6-8-10 here) saves time. The hypotenuse is always the longest side and always opposite the right angle, so an answer smaller than 8 cm must be wrong.","reforge":{"stem":"A right-angled triangle has a hypotenuse of 13 cm and one shorter side of 5 cm. Find the other side.","options":{"A":"12 cm","B":"18 cm","C":"8 cm","D":"14 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-20","stem":"What is the area of a triangle with base 12 cm and perpendicular height 7 cm?","options":{"A":"84 cm²","B":"38 cm²","C":"19 cm²","D":"42 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-AREA2","scaffold":"Area of a triangle = ½ × base × perpendicular height = ½ × 12 × 7 = 42 cm². Forgetting the half gives 84, which is option A. The height must be perpendicular to the chosen base, not a slanted side. Related formulae: parallelogram = base × height; trapezium = ½(a + b) × h. Area is always in square units.","reforge":{"stem":"A trapezium has parallel sides of 5 cm and 9 cm and a height of 6 cm. What is its area?","options":{"A":"270 cm²","B":"42 cm²","C":"84 cm²","D":"27 cm²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-21","stem":"A circle has radius 5 cm. What is its area, in terms of π?","options":{"A":"25π cm²","B":"100π cm²","C":"10π cm²","D":"5π cm²"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLE","scaffold":"Area of a circle = πr² = π × 5² = 25π cm². Circumference = 2πr = 10π cm, which is option A — mixing the two formulae is the commonest error, so note that area uses the square and gives square units. If the diameter is given, halve it first: a circle of diameter 10 cm also has area 25π cm².","reforge":{"stem":"A circle has a diameter of 14 cm. What is its circumference, in terms of π?","options":{"A":"49π cm","B":"196π cm","C":"14π cm","D":"28π cm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-22","stem":"A cuboid measures 4 cm by 5 cm by 6 cm. What is its volume?","options":{"A":"148 cm³","B":"120 cm³","C":"15 cm³","D":"74 cm³"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-VOLUME","scaffold":"Volume of a cuboid = length × width × height = 4 × 5 × 6 = 120 cm³. Surface area is different: 2(4×5 + 5×6 + 4×6) = 2(20 + 30 + 24) = 148 cm², which is option B — check whether the question asks for volume (cubic units) or surface area (square units). For a prism generally, volume = cross-sectional area × length.","reforge":{"stem":"A cylinder has radius 3 cm and height 10 cm. What is its volume, in terms of π?","options":{"A":"30π cm³","B":"900π cm³","C":"60π cm³","D":"90π cm³"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-23","stem":"A fair six-sided dice is rolled once. What is the probability of scoring a number greater than 4?","options":{"A":"1/6","B":"1/2","C":"1/3","D":"2/3"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SIMPLEPROB","scaffold":"Numbers greater than 4 are 5 and 6, so 2 outcomes out of 6, giving 2/6 = 1/3. Listing the successful outcomes explicitly avoids the frequent error of including 4 itself, which would give 1/2. Probabilities always lie between 0 and 1, and the probabilities of all possible outcomes sum to 1, so P(not greater than 4) = 2/3.","reforge":{"stem":"The probability that it rains is 0.3. What is the probability that it does not rain?","options":{"A":"0.7","B":"1.3","C":"0.5","D":"0.3"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-24","stem":"A coin is flipped twice. What is the probability of getting two heads?","options":{"A":"1/2","B":"3/4","C":"1/3","D":"1/4"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBRULES","scaffold":"For independent events, multiply the probabilities: ½ × ½ = ¼. Listing the sample space HH, HT, TH, TT confirms one favourable outcome in four. Use 'and' for multiply and 'or' for add, taking care that adding only applies to mutually exclusive outcomes. With dependent events, such as drawing without replacement, the second probability must be adjusted.","reforge":{"stem":"A bag has 5 red and 3 blue balls. Two are drawn without replacement. P(both red) is:","options":{"A":"25/64","B":"5/14","C":"15/56","D":"5/8"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N1-25","stem":"Find the mean of 4, 7, 9, 12 and 13.","options":{"A":"9","B":"9.5","C":"45","D":"8"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-MEAN2","scaffold":"Mean = total ÷ how many = (4 + 7 + 9 + 12 + 13) ÷ 5 = 45 ÷ 5 = 9. The median is the middle value once ordered, here also 9; the mode is the most frequent, and there is none here; the range is 13 − 4 = 9, a measure of spread rather than average. Answer C is the total, so read whether the mean or the sum is wanted.","reforge":{"stem":"Five numbers have a mean of 12. Four of them are 10, 11, 13 and 14. What is the fifth?","options":{"A":"14","B":"48","C":"12","D":"11"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N4-01","stem":"Work out 3² + 4 × 2.","options":{"A":"26","B":"17","C":"22","D":"13"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-NUMBER","scaffold":"Follow the order of operations: indices first, then multiplication and division, then addition and subtraction. So 3² = 9, then 4 × 2 = 8, and 9 + 8 = 17. Working strictly left to right gives (9 + 4) × 2 = 26, which is option A and the commonest error. Brackets override everything, so (3² + 4) × 2 would indeed be 26.","reforge":{"stem":"Work out 20 − 6 ÷ 2.","options":{"A":"7","B":"14","C":"13","D":"17"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"MATH-N4-02","stem":"Write 84 as a product of its prime factors.","options":{"A":"2 × 42","B":"4 × 21","C":"2² × 3 × 7","D":"2 × 3 × 14"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-FACTORS","scaffold":"Divide repeatedly by the smallest prime that fits: 84 ÷ 2 = 42, 42 ÷ 2 = 21, 21 ÷ 3 = 7, and 7 is prime. So 84 = 2 × 2 × 3 × 7 = 2² × 3 × 7. Every factor in the final answer must itself be prime, which is why the other options fail — 42, 4, 21 and 14 are all composite. Prime factorisation is the basis of finding HCF and LCM.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these numbers is prime?","options":{"A":"29","B":"57","C":"91","D":"51"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH1-01","stem":"Write 0.00072 in standard form. (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"0.00036","B":"3,600,000","C":"72 × 10⁻⁴, which is not standard form since the coefficient must be at least 1 and less than 10.","D":"7.2 × 10⁻⁴"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-NUMBER","scaffold":"The first significant digit is 7 and the decimal moves four places.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: Write 0.00072 in standard form. (alternative exam formulation)","options":{"A":"7.2 × 10⁻⁴","B":"(x + 2)(x + 6)","C":"36,000","D":"8"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH1-02","stem":"Solve x + y = 11 and x − y = 3.","options":{"A":"x = 7, y = 4","B":"x = 4, y = 7","C":"x = 8, y = 3","D":"x = 6, y = 5"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-SIMULTANEOUS","scaffold":"Adding the equations gives 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 4.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? Solve x + y = 11 and x − y = 3.","options":{"A":"x = 4, y = 5","B":"x = 7, y = 4","C":"x = 3, y = 5","D":"x = 6, y = 1"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p1"}]},"GCSE-MATH-P2":{"label":"Paper 2 — Calculator","color":"#1d4ed8","questions":[{"id":"MATH-P2-01","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"Calculate 3.7 × 10⁴ + 2.6 × 10³.","options":{"A":"3.96 × 10⁴","B":"6.30 × 10⁴","C":"6.30 × 10³","D":"6.30 × 10⁷"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-STANDARD2","scaffold":"Rewrite 2.6 × 10³ as 0.26 × 10⁴, then add: 3.7 + 0.26 = 3.96, so the answer is 3.96 × 10⁴.","reforge":{"stem":"What is 5.4 × 10⁶ ÷ 9 × 10²?","options":{"A":"6 × 10²","B":"6 × 10⁴","C":"6 × 10³","D":"6 × 10⁸"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-02","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A right-angled triangle has opposite 7 cm and hypotenuse 12 cm. Find the angle θ.","options":{"A":"30.1°","B":"35.7°","C":"54.3°","D":"59.9°"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIG","scaffold":"Use sin θ = opposite ÷ hypotenuse = 7/12. Therefore θ = sin⁻¹(7/12) ≈ 35.7°.","reforge":{"stem":"A right triangle has adjacent 9 cm and hypotenuse 15 cm. Find the acute angle.","options":{"A":"36.9°","B":"40.0°","C":"59.0°","D":"53.1°"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-03","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A car worth £18,000 depreciates by 12% each year. Find its value after two years.","options":{"A":"£15,840.00","B":"£14,080.00","C":"£13,939.20","D":"£16,920.00"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-COMPOUND","scaffold":"Apply the multiplier 0.88 twice: 18,000 × 0.88² = £13,939.20.","reforge":{"stem":"An investment of £2400 grows by 5% per year. Find its value after three years.","options":{"A":"£2,778.60","B":"£2,772.00","C":"£2,520.00","D":"£2,760.00"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-04","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"Solve x² − 5x − 4 = 0, giving answers to 2 decimal places.","options":{"A":"−5/2","B":"x = 5.00 or −4.00","C":"x = 0.70 or −5.70","D":"x = 5.70 or −0.70"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC","scaffold":"Using the quadratic formula gives x = (5 ± √41)/2, so x ≈ 5.70 or −0.70.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 2x² + 3x − 7 = 0 to 2 decimal places.","options":{"A":"x = 2.77 or −1.27","B":"x = 1.27 or −2.77","C":"x = 1.77 or −2.27","D":"x = 0.77 or −3.27"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-05","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"The equation of a line is y = 3x − 4. What is its gradient?","options":{"A":"3","B":"−4","C":"4","D":"1/3"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-LINEARGRAPH","scaffold":"In y = mx + c, m is the gradient and c is the y-intercept. Here m = 3.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the y-intercept of y = −2x + 7?","options":{"A":"−2","B":"2","C":"7","D":"−7"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-06","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A cylinder has radius 4 cm and height 10 cm. Find its volume to 3 significant figures.","options":{"A":"126 cm³","B":"503 cm³","C":"251 cm³","D":"1005 cm³"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-VOLUME","scaffold":"Volume = πr²h = π × 4² × 10 = 160π ≈ 503 cm³.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the volume of a sphere with radius 3 cm to 3 significant figures.","options":{"A":"28.3 cm³","B":"36.0 cm³","C":"339 cm³","D":"113 cm³"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-07","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A bag contains 5 red and 3 blue counters. Two are taken without replacement. Find P(two red).","options":{"A":"5/8","B":"25/64","C":"5/14","D":"15/56"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY2","scaffold":"The probability is 5/8 × 4/7 = 20/56 = 5/14.","reforge":{"stem":"A box has 4 green and 6 yellow counters. Two are taken without replacement. Find P(two yellow).","options":{"A":"1/3","B":"3/10","C":"1/2","D":"2/5"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-08","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"The first quartile is 18 and the third quartile is 41. Find the interquartile range.","options":{"A":"Mean","B":"59","C":"29","D":"23"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-IQR","scaffold":"Interquartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile = 41 − 18 = 23.","reforge":{"stem":"What does the interquartile range measure?","options":{"A":"31","B":"The spread of the middle half","C":"The most common value","D":"The difference between mean and mode"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-09","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A frequency table has values 2, 4 and 7 with frequencies 3, 5 and 2. Find the mean.","options":{"A":"4.0","B":"4.1","C":"3.8","D":"4.7"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-FREQUENCY","scaffold":"Total frequency is 10 and the total of fx is 6 + 20 + 14 = 40, so the mean is 4.0.","reforge":{"stem":"Values 1, 3 and 8 have frequencies 4, 2 and 4. Find the mean.","options":{"A":"3.6","B":"4.0","C":"4.2","D":"4.8"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-10","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A point is enlarged by scale factor 3 about the origin. What happens to its distance from the origin?","options":{"A":"It is divided by 3.0","B":"It is multiplied by 3","C":"It is unchanged","D":"It is increased by 9.0"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-TRANSFORM","scaffold":"An enlargement with centre at the origin multiplies both coordinates and therefore the distance from the origin by the scale factor 3.","reforge":{"stem":"What does a scale factor −1 do about the origin?","options":{"A":"A reflection in the x-axis only","B":"A translation right","C":"An enlargement by 1","D":"A reflection in the origin"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-11","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A length is 8.0 cm correct to the nearest centimetre. What is its upper bound?","options":{"A":"7.5 cm","B":"8.0 cm","C":"8.5 cm","D":"9.0 cm"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-BOUNDS","scaffold":"Rounding to the nearest centimetre means the true length lies from 7.5 cm inclusive to less than 8.5 cm, so the upper bound is 8.5 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"A mass is 3.2 kg correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. What is the lower bound?","options":{"A":"3.15 kg","B":"3.20 kg","C":"3.25 kg","D":"3.10 kg"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-12","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A regular polygon has exterior angle 24°. How many sides does it have?","options":{"A":"12","B":"24","C":"18","D":"15"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-POLYGON","scaffold":"Exterior angles total 360°, so the number of sides is 360 ÷ 24 = 15.","reforge":{"stem":"Each interior angle of a regular polygon is 150°. How many sides does it have?","options":{"A":"8","B":"12","C":"10","D":"15"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-13","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A ladder 5 m long leans against a wall with its foot 3 m away. How high does it reach?","options":{"A":"4 m","B":"3 m","C":"5.8 m","D":"8 m"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PYTHAGORAS","scaffold":"Use Pythagoras: height² = 5² − 3² = 16, so height = 4 m.","reforge":{"stem":"A rectangle has diagonal 13 cm and width 5 cm. Find its length.","options":{"A":"16 cm","B":"10 cm","C":"12 cm","D":"18 cm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-14","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A price of £240 is reduced by 15%. Find the sale price.","options":{"A":"£216","B":"£204","C":"£225","D":"£276"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-DECIMALPERCENT","scaffold":"The multiplier for a 15% reduction is 0.85: £240 × 0.85 = £204.","reforge":{"stem":"A salary of £28,000 increases by 3%. Find the new salary.","options":{"A":"£28,030","B":"£30,800","C":"£29,000","D":"£28,840"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-15","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A measurement is 4.6 cm correct to the nearest millimetre. State its error interval.","options":{"A":"4.59 ≤ x < 4.61","B":"4.5 ≤ x < 4.7","C":"4.55 ≤ x < 4.65","D":"4.6 ≤ x < 4.7"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-ERROR","scaffold":"Nearest millimetre means half a millimetre either side: 4.55 ≤ x < 4.65 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"A length is 12 cm correct to the nearest centimetre. What is its lower bound?","options":{"A":"11.5 cm","B":"11.9 cm","C":"12.0 cm","D":"12.5 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-16","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"The equation 2x + 1 = 7 is transformed by multiplying both sides by 3. What equation results?","options":{"A":"6x + 3 = 7","B":"6x + 1 = 21","C":"2x + 3 = 21","D":"6x + 3 = 21"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-ALGEBRAPRAC","scaffold":"Multiply every term on both sides by 3: 3(2x + 1) = 3(7), giving 6x + 3 = 21.","reforge":{"stem":"Expand (x + 4)(x − 2).","options":{"A":"x² − 2x + 8","B":"x² + 2x − 8","C":"x² + 6x − 8","D":"x² − 6x + 8"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-17","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A scatter diagram shows a strong positive correlation. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"Positive trend","B":"Causation proven","C":"Equal values","D":"No pattern"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-SCATTER","scaffold":"Positive correlation means the variables tend to rise together. Correlation alone does not prove causation.","reforge":{"stem":"What is an outlier on a scatter diagram?","options":{"A":"Outlier","B":"Nearest point","C":"Atypical","D":"Highest value"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-18","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A cumulative frequency graph reaches 42 at the upper quartile. What does this tell you?","options":{"A":"25% at or below","B":"75% at or below","C":"Mean is 42","D":"All values are 42"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-CUMFREQ","scaffold":"The upper quartile is the value below which about 75% of observations lie.","reforge":{"stem":"What percentage of data lies between the lower and upper quartiles?","options":{"A":"Lower 25%","B":"All data","C":"Upper 75%","D":"50%"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-19","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A transformation maps (x, y) to (x, −y). What is it?","options":{"A":"Rotation 90°","B":"Reflection in the y-axis","C":"Reflection in the x-axis","D":"Translation down"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-TRANSFORM2","scaffold":"The x-coordinate stays the same and the y-coordinate changes sign, which is reflection in the x-axis.","reforge":{"stem":"What matrix represents a reflection in the y-axis?","options":{"A":"[−1 0; 0 1]","B":"[1 0; 0 −1]","C":"[0 1; 1 0]","D":"[1 1; 0 1]"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-20","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A sequence has first term 3 and common ratio 2. Find its fifth term.","options":{"A":"24","B":"405","C":"96","D":"48"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-GEOMETRIC","scaffold":"The terms are 3, 6, 12, 24, 48, so the fifth term is 48.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the common ratio of 5, 15, 45, 135, …?","options":{"A":"2","B":"3","C":"5","D":"10"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-21","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"Calculate (3.2 × 10⁵)(4 × 10²).","options":{"A":"1.28 × 10⁸","B":"12.8 × 10⁷","C":"1.28 × 10⁷","D":"7.2 × 10⁷"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-STANDARD2","scaffold":"Multiply the numbers and add the powers: 3.2 × 4 × 10⁷ = 1.28 × 10⁸.","reforge":{"stem":"Calculate (6 × 10⁷) ÷ (2 × 10³).","options":{"A":"12 × 10⁴","B":"3 × 10¹⁰","C":"3 × 10⁴","D":"4 × 10⁴"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-22","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"Solve 3x² = 75.","options":{"A":"x = 25 or −25","B":"x = 5 or −5","C":"x = 5","D":"x = 75"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC3","scaffold":"x² = 25, so x = 5 or −5.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve x² + 4x − 12 = 0.","options":{"A":"x = 4 or −3","B":"x = 6 or −2","C":"x = 3 or −4","D":"x = 2 or −6"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-23","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A car travels 84 miles in 1.5 hours. Find its average speed.","options":{"A":"126 mph","B":"84 mph","C":"56 mph","D":"42 mph"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SPEED","scaffold":"84 ÷ 1.5 = 56 mph.","reforge":{"stem":"A cyclist travels at 12 m/s for 25 seconds. Find the distance.","options":{"A":"300 m","B":"37 m","C":"0.48 m","D":"13 m"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-24","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A sector has radius 9 cm and angle 80°. Find its area to 3 significant figures.","options":{"A":"254 cm²","B":"113 cm²","C":"20.0 cm²","D":"56.5 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SECTOR","scaffold":"80/360 × π × 9² ≈ 56.5 cm².","reforge":{"stem":"Find the arc length of a 120° sector with radius 6 cm.","options":{"A":"6.28 cm","B":"12.6 cm","C":"18.8 cm","D":"37.7 cm"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-25","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A sphere has volume 500 cm³. Which equation finds its radius r?","options":{"A":"4/3πr³ = 500","B":"x = 4.56 or −0.88","C":"2πr = 500","D":"4πr = 500"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-SPHERE","scaffold":"The sphere volume formula is 4/3πr³.","reforge":{"stem":"Which expression gives the surface area of a sphere of radius r?","options":{"A":"2πr","B":"4/3πr³","C":"4πr²","D":"πr²"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-26","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"The probability of a faulty item is 0.04. Find the probability of a good item.","options":{"A":"0.04","B":"0.96","C":"1.04","D":"0.40"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-COMPLEMENT","scaffold":"The complementary probability is 1 − 0.04 = 0.96.","reforge":{"stem":"If P(A) = 0.72, find P(not A).","options":{"A":"0.07","B":"0.72","C":"1.72","D":"0.28"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-27","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A scatter diagram has correlation coefficient close to −1. What does this suggest?","options":{"A":"Strong positive correlation","B":"No relationship","C":"Strong negative correlation","D":"Causation is certain"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SCATTER","scaffold":"A coefficient near −1 indicates a strong negative linear relationship, not causation.","reforge":{"stem":"Which value suggests the strongest positive correlation?","options":{"A":"0.92","B":"−0.92","C":"0.08","D":"−0.08"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-28","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A histogram has class width 5 and frequency density 6. Find the frequency.","options":{"A":"6","B":"11","C":"1.2","D":"30"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-HISTOGRAM","scaffold":"Frequency = class width × density = 5 × 6 = 30.","reforge":{"stem":"A histogram class has frequency 24 and width 8. Find its density.","options":{"A":"16","B":"3","C":"32","D":"192"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-29","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"Twelve employees have mean salary £28 000. Find the total salary bill.","options":{"A":"£336 000","B":"£40 000","C":"£28 012","D":"£224 000"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-MEAN2","scaffold":"Total = mean × number = 28 000 × 12 = £336 000.","reforge":{"stem":"The mean of 8 values is 14.5. Find their total.","options":{"A":"108","B":"22.5","C":"116","D":"14.5"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-30","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A tree is 12 m tall and its shadow is 7 m long. Find the sun's angle of elevation.","options":{"A":"30.3°","B":"59.7°","C":"35.7°","D":"61.0°"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIG","scaffold":"tan θ = 12/7, so θ ≈ 59.7°.","reforge":{"stem":"A 40 m kite string makes angle 38° with the ground. Find its height.","options":{"A":"20.0 m","B":"31.5 m","C":"49.2 m","D":"24.6 m"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-31","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A recipe uses 250 g flour for 10 cakes. How much is needed for 18 cakes?","options":{"A":"500 g","B":"400 g","C":"450 g","D":"225 g"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-RATIO2","scaffold":"250 ÷ 10 × 18 = 450 g.","reforge":{"stem":"A car uses 7 litres for 100 km. How much for 350 km?","options":{"A":"24.5 litres","B":"x = 5.00 or −4.00","C":"20 litres","D":"14 litres"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-32","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 6, y = 18. Find y when x = 10.","options":{"A":"3","B":"24","C":"12","D":"30"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-PROPORTION","scaffold":"y/x = 3, so y = 3 × 10 = 30.","reforge":{"stem":"y is inversely proportional to x. When x = 4, y = 9. Find y when x = 12.","options":{"A":"27","B":"3","C":"13","D":"36"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-33","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A length is 7.4 cm correct to the nearest millimetre. What is its upper bound?","options":{"A":"7.45 cm","B":"7.40 cm","C":"7.50 cm","D":"7.35 cm"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-BOUNDS2","scaffold":"The upper bound is 7.45 cm, not included.","reforge":{"stem":"A mass is 2.8 kg correct to the nearest 0.1 kg. Find its upper bound.","options":{"A":"2.90 kg","B":"2.80 kg","C":"2.85 kg","D":"2.75 kg"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-34","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"y = 2x + 1 is transformed to y = 2x − 5. What is the transformation?","options":{"A":"Translation up 6","B":"Translation down 6","C":"Translation right 6","D":"Reflection in x-axis"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-GRAPHTRANS","scaffold":"The intercept falls by 6, so translate down 6.","reforge":{"stem":"What maps y = x² to y = (x − 3)²?","options":{"A":"Reflection in y-axis","B":"Translation 3 left","C":"Translation 3 up","D":"Translation 3 right"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-35","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A regular polygon has 12 sides. Find each exterior angle.","options":{"A":"15°","B":"150°","C":"30°","D":"12°"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-POLYGON","scaffold":"360 ÷ 12 = 30°.","reforge":{"stem":"A regular polygon has exterior angle 40°. How many sides?","options":{"A":"9","B":"8","C":"10","D":"12"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-36","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A quadratic graph crosses the x-axis at x = 2 and x = 5. Which equation could represent it?","options":{"A":"y = x² + 7","B":"y = (x + 2)(x + 5)","C":"y = (x − 2)(x + 5)","D":"y = (x − 2)(x − 5)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATICGRAPH","scaffold":"The roots give factors (x − 2) and (x − 5).","reforge":{"stem":"Which are the roots of y = (x + 1)(x − 4)?","options":{"A":"x = 1 and −4","B":"x = −1 and 4","C":"x = −1 and −4","D":"x = 1 and 4"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-37","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A journey has 45 minutes of stops in 3 hours. What percentage is spent stopped?","options":{"A":"25%","B":"15%","C":"20%","D":"30%"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PERCENT2","scaffold":"45/180 × 100 = 25%.","reforge":{"stem":"A student scores 42 out of 60. What percentage is this?","options":{"A":"72%","B":"60%","C":"70%","D":"80%"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-38","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A sample has 34 adults out of 80. Estimate adults in a population of 600.","options":{"A":"240","B":"255","C":"300","D":"204"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-SAMPLING","scaffold":"34/80 × 600 = 255.","reforge":{"stem":"18 of 50 surveyed prefer A. Estimate the number in a group of 800.","options":{"A":"450","B":"360","C":"180","D":"288"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-39","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"u₁ = 4 and uₙ₊₁ = uₙ + 3. Find u₅.","options":{"A":"19","B":"13","C":"16","D":"12"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-RECURRING","scaffold":"The terms are 4, 7, 10, 13, 16.","reforge":{"stem":"A sequence starts 2 and follows uₙ₊₁ = 2uₙ. Find u₄.","options":{"A":"16","B":"8","C":"12","D":"32"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-P2-40","spec":"1MA1 Paper 2","stem":"A tangent from an external point is 12 cm long. Find the other tangent from that point.","options":{"A":"144 cm","B":"6 cm","C":"24 cm","D":"12 cm"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TANGENT","scaffold":"Tangents from the same external point are equal.","reforge":{"stem":"A radius meets a tangent at the point of contact. What is their angle?","options":{"A":"45°","B":"90°","C":"180°","D":"It varies"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-01","stem":"A car travels 150 km in 2.5 hours. What is its average speed?","options":{"A":"60 km/h","B":"375 km/h","C":"37.5 km/h","D":"75 km/h"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-SPEED","scaffold":"Speed = distance ÷ time = 150 ÷ 2.5 = 60 km/h. The formula triangle gives distance = speed × time and time = distance ÷ speed. Units must be consistent: a time given in minutes must be converted to hours before dividing, so 90 minutes is 1.5 hours, not 1.30. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 375, which is option A.","reforge":{"stem":"A train travels at 80 km/h for 45 minutes. How far does it go?","options":{"A":"36 km","B":"3600 km","C":"60 km","D":"106.7 km"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-02","stem":"An object has mass 240 g and volume 30 cm³. What is its density?","options":{"A":"7200 g/cm³","B":"8 g/cm³","C":"0.125 g/cm³","D":"270 g/cm³"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-COMPOUND","scaffold":"Density = mass ÷ volume = 240 ÷ 30 = 8 g/cm³. Rearranging gives mass = density × volume and volume = mass ÷ density. Pressure works the same way: pressure = force ÷ area, in N/m² or pascals. In every compound measure, check that units match the answer required — a density in kg/m³ needs mass in kg and volume in m³.","reforge":{"stem":"A force of 200 N acts on an area of 0.5 m². What is the pressure?","options":{"A":"100 N/m²","B":"200.5 N/m²","C":"0.0025 N/m²","D":"400 N/m²"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-03","stem":"£3000 is invested at 4% compound interest per year. What is the value after 3 years, to the nearest pound?","options":{"A":"£3360","B":"£3120","C":"£3374","D":"£3374.59"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-EXPONENTIAL","scaffold":"Compound interest uses a multiplier raised to the number of periods: 3000 × 1.04³ = 3000 × 1.124864 = £3374.59, which is £3374 to the nearest pound. Simple interest would add 4% of the original each year, giving 3000 + 360 = £3360 — option A, and always less than compound over more than one year. For depreciation use a multiplier below 1, such as 0.85 for a 15% annual fall.","reforge":{"stem":"A car worth £12,000 depreciates by 20% each year. What is it worth after 2 years?","options":{"A":"£7680","B":"£9600","C":"£7200","D":"£4800"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-04","stem":"In a right-angled triangle the side opposite a 35° angle is 8 cm. The hypotenuse is:","options":{"A":"4.59 cm","B":"11.4 cm","C":"9.77 cm","D":"13.9 cm"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIG","scaffold":"Label the sides relative to the given angle, then use SOHCAHTOA. Here we have the opposite and want the hypotenuse, so use sine: sin 35° = 8 ÷ h, giving h = 8 ÷ sin 35° = 8 ÷ 0.5736 = 13.9 cm. Multiplying instead of dividing gives 4.59 cm. Check plausibility: the hypotenuse is the longest side, so it must exceed 8 cm — which rules out option A at once.","reforge":{"stem":"In a right-angled triangle the adjacent side is 12 cm and the hypotenuse is 15 cm. The angle between them is:","options":{"A":"53.1°","B":"36.9°","C":"38.7°","D":"51.3°"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-05","stem":"A triangle has sides 7 cm and 9 cm with an included angle of 40°. Its area is:","options":{"A":"20.2 cm²","B":"31.5 cm²","C":"48.3 cm²","D":"63 cm²"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIANGLERULES","scaffold":"Area = ½ab sin C, using two sides and the angle between them: ½ × 7 × 9 × sin 40° = 31.5 × 0.6428 = 20.2 cm². Option A is what you get by omitting the sine. Use the sine rule when you have a matching side and opposite angle, and the cosine rule when you have three sides, or two sides with the angle between them and need the third side.","reforge":{"stem":"A triangle has sides 5 cm and 8 cm with an included angle of 60°. The third side is:","options":{"A":"9.85 cm","B":"11.4 cm","C":"7 cm","D":"13 cm"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-06","stem":"A bearing is measured from north. What is the bearing of due west?","options":{"A":"090°","B":"270°","C":"360°","D":"180°"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-BEARINGS","scaffold":"Bearings are measured clockwise from north and always written with three figures: north 000°, east 090°, south 180°, west 270°. A back bearing is found by adding 180° if the original is below 180°, or subtracting 180° if it is above. So if B is on a bearing of 070° from A, then A is on a bearing of 250° from B.","reforge":{"stem":"B is on a bearing of 125° from A. What is the bearing of A from B?","options":{"A":"235°","B":"055°","C":"245°","D":"305°"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-07","stem":"A map has a scale of 1:25,000. A distance of 4 cm on the map represents what real distance?","options":{"A":"10 km","B":"100 m","C":"1 km","D":"6.25 km"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SCALE","scaffold":"A scale of 1:25,000 means 1 cm on the map is 25,000 cm in reality. So 4 cm represents 100,000 cm. Convert: 100,000 cm ÷ 100 = 1000 m = 1 km. The conversions to keep straight are 100 cm in a metre and 1000 m in a kilometre, so 100,000 cm in a kilometre. Working in the wrong direction — dividing by the scale rather than multiplying — is the usual error.","reforge":{"stem":"On a 1:50,000 map, how many centimetres represent a real distance of 3 km?","options":{"A":"6 cm","B":"60 cm","C":"15 cm","D":"1.5 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-08","stem":"Two similar triangles have corresponding sides of 6 cm and 9 cm. The smaller has area 20 cm². The area of the larger is:","options":{"A":"30 cm²","B":"180 cm²","C":"67.5 cm²","D":"45 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TRANSFORM2","scaffold":"For similar shapes the length scale factor is 9 ÷ 6 = 1.5. Areas scale by the square of that factor, so 1.5² = 2.25, giving 20 × 2.25 = 45 cm². Volumes scale by the cube, 1.5³ = 3.375. Multiplying the area by the length factor alone gives 30 cm², option A, and is the commonest error in this topic.","reforge":{"stem":"Two similar solids have heights in the ratio 2:3. The smaller has volume 16 cm³. The larger has volume:","options":{"A":"24 cm³","B":"54 cm³","C":"36 cm³","D":"108 cm³"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-09","stem":"Solve x² − 5x + 6 = 0.","options":{"A":"x = 2 or x = 3","B":"x = −2 or x = −3","C":"x = 1 or x = 6","D":"x = 5 or x = 6"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC","scaffold":"Factorise by finding two numbers that multiply to +6 and add to −5: those are −2 and −3, giving (x − 2)(x − 3) = 0, so x = 2 or x = 3. Note the signs: the roots are the values that make each bracket zero, so a bracket of (x − 2) gives a root of +2. Substituting back is the check: 4 − 10 + 6 = 0 and 9 − 15 + 6 = 0.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve x² + 2x − 8 = 0.","options":{"A":"x = 2 or x = 4","B":"x = −2 or x = 4","C":"x = 2 or x = −4","D":"x = −2 or x = −4"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-10","stem":"Using the quadratic formula, the discriminant of 2x² + 3x + 5 = 0 is:","options":{"A":"−49","B":"−31","C":"9","D":"31"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC2","scaffold":"The discriminant is b² − 4ac, here 3² − 4(2)(5) = 9 − 40 = −31. Because it is negative there are no real roots, so the parabola never crosses the x-axis. A discriminant of zero means one repeated root and the curve touches the axis; a positive value means two distinct roots. Sign errors when a or c is negative are the usual difficulty.","reforge":{"stem":"A quadratic has discriminant equal to 0. This means the equation has:","options":{"A":"No real roots at all","B":"Two distinct real roots","C":"Three real roots","D":"One repeated real root"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-11","stem":"A straight line passes through (0, 3) with gradient 2. Its equation is:","options":{"A":"y = 3x + 2","B":"y = 2x − 3","C":"y = 2x + 3","D":"y = 3x − 2"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-LINEARGRAPH","scaffold":"A straight line has equation y = mx + c, where m is the gradient and c the y-intercept. With gradient 2 and intercept 3 the equation is y = 2x + 3. Swapping m and c gives option A, a frequent slip. Gradient between two points is (change in y) ÷ (change in x). Parallel lines share a gradient; perpendicular gradients multiply to −1.","reforge":{"stem":"A line is perpendicular to y = 4x − 1. Its gradient is:","options":{"A":"−1/4","B":"4","C":"0.25","D":"−4"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-12","stem":"Find the gradient of the line joining (1, 4) and (5, 16).","options":{"A":"4","B":"1/3","C":"12","D":"3"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-COORDINATES","scaffold":"Gradient = (y₂ − y₁) ÷ (x₂ − x₁) = (16 − 4) ÷ (5 − 1) = 12 ÷ 4 = 3. Take the coordinates in the same order top and bottom, or the sign will be wrong. The midpoint of the same two points is ((1+5)/2, (4+16)/2) = (3, 10), and the length is found by Pythagoras: √(4² + 12²) = √160.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the midpoint of the line joining (2, 7) and (8, 3)?","options":{"A":"(3, 2)","B":"(5, 5)","C":"(6, 4)","D":"(10, 10)"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-13","stem":"Solve the inequality 3x + 4 < 19.","options":{"A":"x < 5","B":"x > 5","C":"x < 7.67","D":"x < 15"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Solve as for an equation: subtract 4 to get 3x < 15, then divide by 3 to get x < 5. The inequality sign only reverses when multiplying or dividing by a negative number, so −2x > 6 becomes x < −3. On a number line, use an open circle for < or > and a filled circle for ≤ or ≥.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve −2x + 1 ≥ 9.","options":{"A":"x ≥ −4","B":"x ≥ 4","C":"x ≤ −4","D":"x ≤ 4"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-14","stem":"y is directly proportional to x. When x = 4, y = 20. What is y when x = 7?","options":{"A":"23","B":"35","C":"11.4","D":"140"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PROPORTION","scaffold":"Direct proportion means y = kx. From x = 4 and y = 20, k = 5, so y = 5x and when x = 7, y = 35. Inverse proportion means y = k/x, so as one doubles the other halves. Adding the difference in x rather than scaling gives 23, option A — proportion is multiplicative, not additive, which is the key distinction being tested.","reforge":{"stem":"y is inversely proportional to x. When x = 3, y = 12. What is y when x = 6?","options":{"A":"24","B":"9","C":"15","D":"6"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-15","stem":"A number is 47 when rounded to the nearest whole number. Its lower bound is:","options":{"A":"47.4","B":"46","C":"46.5","D":"46.9"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-BOUNDS","scaffold":"Rounding to the nearest whole number means the value lies within half a unit either side: the lower bound is 46.5 and the upper bound 47.5. The upper bound is written as 47.5 even though a value exactly 47.5 would round up, because it is the limit of the interval. For a value given to 1 decimal place, the bounds are ±0.05; to the nearest 10, ±5.","reforge":{"stem":"A length is 8.4 cm to 1 decimal place. Its upper bound is:","options":{"A":"8.45 cm","B":"8.5 cm","C":"8.49 cm","D":"8.44 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-16","stem":"Estimate the value of (39.2 × 5.1) ÷ 1.97 by rounding each number to 1 significant figure.","options":{"A":"200","B":"120","C":"20","D":"100"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-ESTIMATION","scaffold":"Round each number to 1 significant figure: 39.2 → 40, 5.1 → 5, 1.97 → 2. Then (40 × 5) ÷ 2 = 200 ÷ 2 = 100. Estimation questions want the rounding shown, not the exact answer, and the point is to check whether a calculator result is plausible. The true value here is 101.5, so the estimate is close.","reforge":{"stem":"Estimate √(48.6 ÷ 3.1) by rounding to 1 significant figure.","options":{"A":"16","B":"4","C":"5","D":"2.5"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-17","stem":"A frequency table records marks. The class 10 < m ≤ 20 has frequency 8. What is used as the midpoint for estimating the mean?","options":{"A":"15","B":"10","C":"8","D":"20"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-FREQUENCY","scaffold":"For grouped data the exact values are unknown, so the class midpoint stands in for them: (10 + 20) ÷ 2 = 15. Estimated mean = Σ(midpoint × frequency) ÷ Σfrequency. The answer is an estimate precisely because the original values are lost. The modal class is the one with the highest frequency, and the class containing the median is found by counting to the middle position.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the mean from a grouped frequency table only an estimate?","options":{"A":"The frequencies may be recorded wrongly","B":"The classes are of unequal width","C":"The exact data values are not known","D":"The total frequency is always approximate"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-18","stem":"A cumulative frequency graph is used to estimate the median of 60 values. Which cumulative frequency should be read off?","options":{"A":"60","B":"30","C":"15","D":"45"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-CUMFREQ","scaffold":"For a cumulative frequency curve the median is read at half the total, here 30. The lower quartile is at a quarter (15) and the upper quartile at three quarters (45), so the interquartile range is UQ − LQ. Cumulative frequency is always plotted against the upper class boundary, not the midpoint — plotting at the midpoint is a common and costly error.","reforge":{"stem":"The lower quartile is 24 and the upper quartile is 39. What is the interquartile range?","options":{"A":"7.5","B":"63","C":"31.5","D":"15"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-19","stem":"A scatter graph shows points falling from top left to bottom right. This shows:","options":{"A":"Positive correlation","B":"No correlation at all","C":"Negative correlation","D":"A causal relationship"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SCATTER","scaffold":"A downward trend from left to right shows negative correlation: as one variable increases the other decreases. Upward shows positive correlation, and scattered points show none. Correlation does not prove causation — a third factor may explain both. A line of best fit can be used to predict within the data range (interpolation); predicting beyond it (extrapolation) is unreliable.","reforge":{"stem":"Using a line of best fit to predict a value far outside the range of the data is unreliable because it is:","options":{"A":"Extrapolation, beyond the evidence","B":"Interpolation, which assumes linearity","C":"Correlation without causation","D":"An estimate of the mean"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-20","stem":"A histogram has a class of width 5 and frequency density 3. What is the frequency of that class?","options":{"A":"1.67","B":"8","C":"3","D":"15"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-HISTOGRAM","scaffold":"In a histogram the area of each bar gives the frequency, so frequency = frequency density × class width = 3 × 5 = 15. Rearranged, frequency density = frequency ÷ class width. Histograms are used when classes have unequal widths, which is why the height is a density rather than the frequency itself — reading the height as the frequency is the standard error.","reforge":{"stem":"A class of width 4 contains 20 values. Its frequency density is:","options":{"A":"80","B":"5","C":"16","D":"24"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-21","stem":"A spinner has 4 equal sections. It is spun 200 times. Approximately how many times would you expect a particular section?","options":{"A":"50","B":"4","C":"200","D":"25"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY2","scaffold":"Expected frequency = probability × number of trials = ¼ × 200 = 50. Relative frequency, found from experimental results, estimates probability and becomes more reliable as the number of trials increases. A result differing from the expectation does not prove bias — random variation is expected — but a large and persistent difference over many trials suggests the spinner is not fair.","reforge":{"stem":"A coin is flipped 100 times and lands heads 56 times. The relative frequency of heads is:","options":{"A":"0.5","B":"56","C":"0.56","D":"0.44"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-22","stem":"Two events are mutually exclusive. P(A) = 0.3 and P(B) = 0.45. What is P(A or B)?","options":{"A":"0.135","B":"0.75","C":"0.15","D":"0.25"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY4","scaffold":"Mutually exclusive events cannot both happen, so P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.3 + 0.45 = 0.75. Multiplying instead gives 0.135, which would apply to independent events both occurring. If events are not mutually exclusive, subtract the overlap: P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) − P(A and B). Any probability above 1 signals an error.","reforge":{"stem":"Events A and B are independent with P(A) = 0.5 and P(B) = 0.2. P(A and B) is:","options":{"A":"0.7","B":"0.3","C":"0.25","D":"0.1"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-23","stem":"A sample is chosen so that every member of the population has an equal chance of selection. This is:","options":{"A":"A stratified sample","B":"A biased sample","C":"A random sample","D":"A systematic sample"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SAMPLING","scaffold":"Random sampling gives every member an equal chance, so the sample should be representative. Stratified sampling divides the population into groups and takes a number from each in proportion to group size. Systematic sampling takes every nth member. A biased sample over-represents part of the population — asking only people leaving a gym about exercise habits is the classic example.","reforge":{"stem":"A school has 600 girls and 400 boys. A stratified sample of 50 should contain how many boys?","options":{"A":"20","B":"30","C":"25","D":"40"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-24","stem":"A shape is enlarged by scale factor 3 about the origin. A point at (2, 4) moves to:","options":{"A":"(5, 7)","B":"(6, 4)","C":"(2/3, 4/3)","D":"(6, 12)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TRANSFORM","scaffold":"An enlargement about the origin multiplies both coordinates by the scale factor: (2, 4) becomes (6, 12). A scale factor between 0 and 1 makes the shape smaller; a negative factor puts the image on the opposite side of the centre and inverts it. Enlargement changes size but not shape, so the image is similar to the object with all angles unchanged.","reforge":{"stem":"A point (5, 2) is translated by the vector (−3, 4). Its image is:","options":{"A":"(8, −2)","B":"(2, 6)","C":"(−15, 8)","D":"(2, −2)"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N2-25","stem":"The vector a = (3, −1) and b = (2, 5). What is a + b?","options":{"A":"(5, 4)","B":"(1, −6)","C":"(6, −5)","D":"(5, −6)"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-VECTORS","scaffold":"Add vectors component by component: (3 + 2, −1 + 5) = (5, 4). Subtracting reverses the second vector's components, and multiplying by a scalar multiplies both. Two vectors are parallel when one is a scalar multiple of the other. The magnitude of (5, 4) is √(5² + 4²) = √41, found by Pythagoras.","reforge":{"stem":"Which vector is parallel to (2, 3)?","options":{"A":"(3, 2)","B":"(−2, 3)","C":"(6, 9)","D":"(4, 5)"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N4-03","stem":"A recipe for 4 people uses 600 g of rice. How much is needed for 6 people?","options":{"A":"800 g","B":"900 g","C":"1000 g","D":"400 g"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PROPORTION","scaffold":"Find the amount for one person, then scale: 600 ÷ 4 = 150 g each, so 6 × 150 = 900 g. Alternatively multiply by the ratio 6/4 = 1.5, giving 600 × 1.5 = 900 g. Adding 200 g because there are 2 more people gives 800 g, option B, and treats a multiplicative relationship as an additive one — the standard error in proportion questions.","reforge":{"stem":"5 identical books weigh 1.2 kg. What do 8 weigh?","options":{"A":"1.5 kg","B":"0.75 kg","C":"2.4 kg","D":"1.92 kg"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"MATH-N4-04","stem":"A shop sells 3 pens for £1.44. What is the cost of 7 pens?","options":{"A":"£4.32","B":"£10.08","C":"£3.36","D":"£2.88"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-MEASURES","scaffold":"Find the unit cost then scale: £1.44 ÷ 3 = £0.48 per pen, so 7 × £0.48 = £3.36. This 'best buy' method also compares deals: work out the price per item or per 100 g for each offer and pick the lower. Multiplying by 7 without first dividing by 3 gives £10.08, which is option B.","reforge":{"stem":"Which is better value: 500 g for £2.00, or 800 g for £3.04?","options":{"A":"The 800 g pack","B":"They cost the same per gram","C":"The 500 g pack","D":"It cannot be compared"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH2-01","stem":"A sector has radius 8 cm and angle 45°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":{"A":"64π cm²","B":"16π cm²","C":"32π cm²","D":"8π cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SECTOR","scaffold":"Sector area is 45/360 × π × 8² = 8π cm².","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: A sector has radius 8 cm and angle 45°. What is its area in terms of π?","options":{"A":"8π cm²","B":"40 + 4π cm","C":"10 + 4π cm","D":"20 + 2π cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH2-02","stem":"A bag contains 4 red and 6 blue counters. Two are taken without replacement. What is P(two blue)?","options":{"A":"1/3","B":"2/5","C":"20/56","D":"25/64"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY2","scaffold":"The probability is 6/10 × 5/9 = 1/3.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? A bag contains 4 red and 6 blue counters. Two are taken without replacement. What is P(two blue)?","options":{"A":"3/10","B":"1/3","C":"6.30 × 10³","D":"6.30 × 10⁷"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p2"}]},"GCSE-MATH-P3":{"label":"Paper 3 — Calculator","color":"#1d4ed8","questions":[{"id":"MATH-P3-01","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"The graph of y = x² − 4x + 1 has a minimum. What is the x-coordinate of the minimum?","options":{"A":"2","B":"y = (x − 2)(x + 5)","C":"x = 1 and −4","D":"y = (x + 2)(x + 5)"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATICGRAPH","scaffold":"Complete the square: y = (x − 2)² − 3, so the minimum occurs when x = 2.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the minimum value of y = (x + 3)² − 5?","options":{"A":"x = 1 and 4","B":"y = x² + 7","C":"−5","D":"x = −1 and −4"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-02","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Solve 2x + 3 < 11.","options":{"A":"−2 and −1","B":"x < 4","C":"x < 7","D":"x > 7"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-INEQUALITY","scaffold":"Subtract 3 to get 2x < 8, then divide by 2: x < 4.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 5 − 2x ≥ 11.","options":{"A":"x ≥ −3","B":"x ≤ 3","C":"x ≥ 3","D":"x ≤ −3"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-03","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"The vectors a = (2, 1) and b = (−1, 3). Find 2a + b.","options":{"A":"(3, 1)","B":"(4, 5)","C":"(3, 5)","D":"(5, 4)"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-VECTORS","scaffold":"2a = (4, 2), so 2a + b = (4, 2) + (−1, 3) = (3, 5).","reforge":{"stem":"If p = (4, −2) and q = (1, 5), find p − q.","options":{"A":"(3, −7)","B":"(5, 3)","C":"(3, 7)","D":"(−3, −7)"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-04","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A circle has centre (2, −1) and radius 5. Which equation represents it?","options":{"A":"(−5, −1)","B":"(x + 2)² + (y − 1)² = 5","C":"(x − 2)² + (y − 1)² = 25","D":"(x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 25"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLE","scaffold":"The circle equation is (x − a)² + (y − b)² = r². With centre (2, −1) and radius 5, this becomes (x − 2)² + (y + 1)² = 25.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the centre of (x + 4)² + (y − 2)² = 49?","options":{"A":"(4, −2)","B":"(−4, 2)","C":"(−4, −2)","D":"(4, 2)"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-05","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A bearing of 135° is measured clockwise from which direction?","options":{"A":"North","B":"East","C":"South","D":"West"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-BEARINGS","scaffold":"Bearings are measured clockwise from north and written as three figures. A bearing of 135° is southeast of the starting point.","reforge":{"stem":"A boat travels on a bearing of 210°. In which general direction does it travel?","options":{"A":"North-east","B":"South-east","C":"South-west","D":"North-west"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-06","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A tangent touches a circle at P. What is the angle between the tangent and the radius OP?","options":{"A":"145°","B":"90°","C":"172°","D":"It depends on the radius"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLETHEOREM","scaffold":"A tangent is perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, so the angle is 90°.","reforge":{"stem":"The angle between a tangent and a chord equals which angle?","options":{"A":"The angle at the centre divided by two only","B":"The central angle","C":"The reflex angle","D":"The angle in the alternate segment"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-07","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A tree is 18 m from a point. The angle of elevation to its top is 32°. Find its height.","options":{"A":"9.54 m","B":"14.28 m","C":"11.25 m","D":"28.80 m"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIG","scaffold":"Use tan 32° = height ÷ 18, so height = 18 tan 32° ≈ 11.25 m.","reforge":{"stem":"A ramp is 4.5 m long and rises 1.2 m. Find its angle to the horizontal.","options":{"A":"15.5°","B":"14.0°","C":"27.5°","D":"74.5°"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-08","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A population grows from 12,000 by 2.4% each year. Which model gives its size after n years?","options":{"A":"12000 × 1.240ⁿ","B":"12000 + 2.4n","C":"12000 × 0.240ⁿ","D":"12000 × 1.024ⁿ"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-EXPONENTIAL","scaffold":"A percentage increase of 2.4% uses multiplier 1.024, so repeated growth is modelled by 12000(1.024)ⁿ.","reforge":{"stem":"A quantity halves every 6 hours. Which model gives its value after t hours from initial value A?","options":{"A":"A(1/2)^(t/3)","B":"A(1/2)^(t/6)","C":"A(1/2)^(6t)","D":"A(2)^(t/6)"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-09","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"The equation x³ − 4x − 1 = 0 has a root between which pair?","options":{"A":"2 and 3","B":"1 and 2","C":"0 and 1","D":"3 and 4"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-ITERATION","scaffold":"Substitution gives f(2) = −1 and f(3) = 14, so the sign change shows a root between 2 and 3.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the first step in using an iteration formula to solve an equation?","options":{"A":"Differentiate the graph","B":"Round every value to an integer","C":"Choose starting value","D":"Ignore the chosen interval"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-10","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A normal distribution has mean 50 and standard deviation 6. What does the mean represent?","options":{"A":"Total spread","B":"Central location","C":"Most extreme value","D":"Observation count"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-NORMAL","scaffold":"The mean is the central location of a normal distribution; the standard deviation describes how spread out values are around it.","reforge":{"stem":"In a normal distribution, approximately what proportion lies within one standard deviation of the mean?","options":{"A":"50%","B":"99.7%","C":"95%","D":"68%"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-11","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"What is the probability of an event that is certain?","options":{"A":"1/6","B":"0.5","C":"1","D":"100"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBABILITY","scaffold":"Probability ranges from 0 to 1. A certain event has probability 1, or 100%.","reforge":{"stem":"If P(A) = 0.37, what is P(not A)?","options":{"A":"0.63","B":"0.37","C":"1.37","D":"2.70"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-12","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A proof starts: n² − n is even for every integer n. Which factorisation helps prove this?","options":{"A":"2n + 1","B":"(n − 1)²","C":"n² + 1","D":"n(n − 1)"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-PROOF","scaffold":"Consecutive integers n and n − 1 include one even number, so their product n(n − 1) is even.","reforge":{"stem":"Why is the product of two consecutive integers always even?","options":{"A":"Both must be prime","B":"One of them must be even","C":"Their sum is always even","D":"Their difference is always odd"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-13","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A proof shows n(n + 1) is even for every integer n. Why?","options":{"A":"One even factor","B":"Both factors prime","C":"Product always odd","D":"n always even"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PROOF","scaffold":"Among any two consecutive integers, one must be even, so their product is divisible by 2.","reforge":{"stem":"Which expression is always divisible by 3 for integer n?","options":{"A":"A square then one","B":"One term plus three","C":"Three terms in row","D":"Twice n then one"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-14","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A vector from A to B is (6, −2). Find the midpoint vector from A to the midpoint of AB.","options":{"A":"(12, −4)","B":"(3, −1)","C":"(6, −1)","D":"(3, −2)"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-VECTORS","scaffold":"The midpoint is halfway along the vector, so halve each component: (6, −2) ÷ 2 = (3, −1).","reforge":{"stem":"What is the magnitude of vector (3, 4)?","options":{"A":"(3, 1)","B":"(4, 5)","C":"(−3, −7)","D":"5"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-15","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A chord is 8 cm from the centre of a circle of radius 10 cm. Find the chord length.","options":{"A":"18 cm","B":"16 cm","C":"12 cm","D":"5.8 m"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PYTHAGORAS","scaffold":"The perpendicular from the centre bisects the chord. Half-length = √(10² − 8²) = 6, so chord length = 12 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"The perpendicular from the centre to a chord is 5 cm in a circle of radius 13 cm. Find the chord length.","options":{"A":"24 cm","B":"12 cm","C":"8 m","D":"3 m"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-16","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A sector has angle 72° and radius 10 cm. Find its perimeter in terms of π.","options":{"A":"40 + 4π cm","B":"10 + 4π cm","C":"20 + 2π cm","D":"20 + 4π cm"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SECTOR","scaffold":"The two radii total 20 cm. The arc is 72/360 × 2π × 10 = 4π cm, so the perimeter is 20 + 4π cm.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the area of a 90° sector with radius 8 cm.","options":{"A":"8π cm²","B":"16π cm²","C":"32π cm²","D":"64π cm²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-17","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"The iteration xₙ₊₁ = (10 + xₙ²)/6 starts with x₀ = 2. Find x₁.","options":{"A":"2.33","B":"1.67","C":"1.00","D":"2.67"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-ITERATION","scaffold":"Substitute x₀ = 2: x₁ = (10 + 2²)/6 = 14/6 ≈ 2.33.","reforge":{"stem":"Why are repeated iteration values rounded only at the end?","options":{"A":"The formula stops working","B":"Rounding increases accuracy","C":"Early rounding can change later values","D":"It removes the need for a starting value"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-18","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"For a normal distribution with mean 100 and standard deviation 15, what is the z-score for 130?","options":{"A":"99.7%","B":"2","C":"2.5","D":"50%"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-NORMAL","scaffold":"z = (value − mean) ÷ standard deviation = (130 − 100)/15 = 2.","reforge":{"stem":"A value has z-score −1.5 in a distribution with mean 40 and standard deviation 8. Find the value.","options":{"A":"Total spread","B":"Observation count","C":"Most extreme value","D":"28"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-19","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Events A and B are mutually exclusive. If P(A)=0.3 and P(B)=0.4, find P(A or B).","options":{"A":"0.1","B":"0.15","C":"0.7","D":"0.28"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBRULES","scaffold":"Mutually exclusive events cannot happen together, so P(A or B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.7.","reforge":{"stem":"If P(A)=0.6 and P(B)=0.5 for independent events, find P(A and B).","options":{"A":"0.3","B":"0.55","C":"1.1","D":"0.1"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-20","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A function is defined by f(x)=2x+1. What is f⁻¹(x)?","options":{"A":"1/(2x + 1)","B":"(x + 1)/2","C":"2x − 1","D":"(x − 1)/2"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SUBSTITUTION","scaffold":"Set y = 2x + 1, swap x and y, then rearrange: x = 2y + 1 gives y = (x − 1)/2.","reforge":{"stem":"What is the composite function fg(x) if f(x)=2x and g(x)=x+3?","options":{"A":"2x + 3","B":"2x + 6","C":"x + 6","D":"2x − 3"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-21","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Solve simultaneously: x + y = 11 and x − y = 3.","options":{"A":"x = 7, y = 4","B":"x = 4, y = 7","C":"x = 8, y = 3","D":"x = 6, y = 5"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-SIMULTANEOUS","scaffold":"Adding gives 2x = 14, so x = 7 and y = 4.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 2x + y = 13 and x − y = 2.","options":{"A":"x = 4, y = 5","B":"x = 3, y = 5","C":"x = 5, y = 3","D":"x = 6, y = 1"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-22","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"x² + 6x + 1 = 0 has roots α and β. Find α + β.","options":{"A":"x = 5.00 or −4.00","B":"−6","C":"x = 0.70 or −5.70","D":"−1"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC","scaffold":"The sum of roots is −b, so α + β = −6.","reforge":{"stem":"For 2x² − 5x − 3 = 0, find the product of the roots.","options":{"A":"−5/2","B":"5/2","C":"3/2","D":"−3/2"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-23","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"In a triangle, a = 8 cm, A = 30° and B = 45°. Find b to 3 significant figures.","options":{"A":"9.24 cm","B":"5.66 cm","C":"11.3 cm","D":"16.0 cm"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIANGLERULES","scaffold":"The sine rule gives b = 8sin45°/sin30° ≈ 11.3 cm.","reforge":{"stem":"Two sides are 7 cm and 9 cm with included angle 60°. Find the opposite side.","options":{"A":"√67 cm","B":"√130 cm","C":"88.0 cm²","D":"16 cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-24","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Find the area of a triangle with sides 8 cm and 11 cm and included angle 40°.","options":{"A":"88.0 cm²","B":"44.0 cm²","C":"35.4 cm²","D":"28.3 cm²"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-TRIANGLERULES","scaffold":"Area = 1/2 × 8 × 11 × sin40° ≈ 28.3 cm².","reforge":{"stem":"A triangle has sides 6 cm and 10 cm with included angle 30°. Find its area.","options":{"A":"30 cm²","B":"15 cm²","C":"17.3 cm²","D":"60 cm²"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-25","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"(x − 3)² + (y + 2)² = 16. Find the radius.","options":{"A":"4","B":"(x + 2)² + (y − 1)² = 5","C":"(−4, −2)","D":"(x − 2)² + (y − 1)² = 25"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLE","scaffold":"r² = 16, so r = 4.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the centre of (x + 5)² + (y − 1)² = 9.","options":{"A":"(−5, −1)","B":"(5, −1)","C":"(−5, 1)","D":"(5, 1)"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-26","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"a = (3, −1). Find the magnitude of 2a.","options":{"A":"(1, −6)","B":"2√10","C":"(−3, −7)","D":"(12, −4)"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-VECTORS","scaffold":"2a = (6, −2), whose magnitude is √40 = 2√10.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the magnitude of vector (5, 12).","options":{"A":"169","B":"17","C":"(6, −1)","D":"13"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-27","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A line is perpendicular to a line with gradient 1/2. Find its gradient.","options":{"A":"−1/2","B":"292°","C":"−2","D":"112°"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PARALLEL","scaffold":"Perpendicular gradients multiply to −1, so the gradient is −2.","reforge":{"stem":"Find the gradient perpendicular to gradient −3.","options":{"A":"1/3","B":"−1/3","C":"137°","D":"223°"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-28","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A bearing of 325° is between which directions?","options":{"A":"West and south-west","B":"South and south-west","C":"East and north-east","D":"North and north-west"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-BEARINGS","scaffold":"325° is 35° west of north.","reforge":{"stem":"A ship travels on bearing 072°. Which general direction is this?","options":{"A":"West-north-west","B":"East-north-east","C":"South-east","D":"North-west"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-29","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A frequency polygon has midpoint 15 and frequency 8. What point is plotted?","options":{"A":"(15, 8)","B":"(8, 15)","C":"(15, 4)","D":"(30, 8)"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-FREQUENCY","scaffold":"Plot frequency against midpoint: (15, 8).","reforge":{"stem":"For 20 < x ≤ 30, find the class midpoint.","options":{"A":"3.8","B":"4.1","C":"25","D":"4.7"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-30","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A cumulative frequency graph has total frequency 80. At what frequency is the median estimated?","options":{"A":"All values are 42","B":"40","C":"Lower 25%","D":"25% at or below"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-CUMFREQ","scaffold":"The median is at half the total frequency: 80 ÷ 2 = 40.","reforge":{"stem":"A cumulative frequency graph has total frequency 120. Where is the lower quartile estimated?","options":{"A":"Mean is 42","B":"Upper 75%","C":"90","D":"30"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-31","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Independent events have P(A)=0.4 and P(B)=0.25. Find P(A and B).","options":{"A":"0.15","B":"0.65","C":"0.10","D":"0.90"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PROBRULES","scaffold":"For independent events, 0.4 × 0.25 = 0.10.","reforge":{"stem":"P(A)=0.3 and P(B)=0.6 for independent events. Find P(A or B).","options":{"A":"0.72","B":"0.90","C":"0.18","D":"0.42"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-32","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A box plot has lower quartile 12 and upper quartile 31. Find its IQR.","options":{"A":"31","B":"43","C":"The difference between mean and mode","D":"19"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-IQR","scaffold":"IQR = 31 − 12 = 19.","reforge":{"stem":"Which measure is shown by the length of the box?","options":{"A":"29","B":"Interquartile range","C":"Mean — a single average value, not the measure of spread shown by the length of the box.","D":"59"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-33","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A cumulative frequency graph has Q1 = 18 and Q3 = 42. Find the IQR.","options":{"A":"24","B":"Mean","C":"59","D":"The difference between mean and mode"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-IQR","scaffold":"IQR = Q3 − Q1 = 42 − 18 = 24.","reforge":{"stem":"A box plot has Q1 = 14 and Q3 = 39. Find its IQR.","options":{"A":"53","B":"The most common value","C":"25","D":"26"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-34","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"xₙ₊₁ = √(12 − xₙ) starts with x₀ = 2. Find x₁.","options":{"A":"It removes the need for a starting value","B":"√10","C":"√14","D":"2√3"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-ITERATION","scaffold":"x₁ = √(12 − 2) = √10.","reforge":{"stem":"For xₙ₊₁ = (xₙ + 6/xₙ)/2 and x₀ = 3, find x₁.","options":{"A":"1.5","B":"3.5","C":"4.5","D":"2.5"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-35","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A sequence has nth term n² + 2. Find the third term.","options":{"A":"n = 9","B":"n = 12","C":"11","D":"58"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-NTH","scaffold":"3² + 2 = 11.","reforge":{"stem":"Which term of n² − 1 equals 24?","options":{"A":"n = 5","B":"n = 4","C":"n = 6","D":"n = 23"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-36","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"A geometric sequence starts 5 with ratio 3. Find the fourth term.","options":{"A":"405","B":"96","C":"60","D":"135"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-GEOMETRIC","scaffold":"The terms are 5, 15, 45, 135.","reforge":{"stem":"A geometric sequence starts 81 with ratio 1/3. Find the third term.","options":{"A":"27","B":"9","C":"3","D":"1"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-37","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"An angle in the alternate segment is 35°. What is the angle between the tangent and chord?","options":{"A":"35°","B":"70°","C":"55°","D":"145°"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLETHEOREM","scaffold":"The alternate segment theorem makes the two angles equal.","reforge":{"stem":"An angle at the centre is 86°. Find the angle at the circumference on the same arc.","options":{"A":"172°","B":"86°","C":"43°","D":"23°"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-38","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"In P = 500(1.08)ⁿ, what does 1.08 represent?","options":{"A":"An 8% decrease multiplier","B":"An 8% growth multiplier","C":"An increase of 1.08 units","D":"The starting value"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-EXPONENTIAL","scaffold":"1.08 means multiplication by 108%, an 8% increase.","reforge":{"stem":"In A = 1200(0.92)ᵗ, what change occurs each period?","options":{"A":"A 0.92% decrease","B":"A 92% decrease","C":"An 8% increase","D":"An 8% decrease"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-39","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"Solve 2ˣ = 32.","options":{"A":"x = 30","B":"x = 16","C":"x = 5","D":"x = 6"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-INDICES","scaffold":"32 = 2⁵, so x = 5.","reforge":{"stem":"Solve 10ˣ = 1000.","options":{"A":"x = 3","B":"x = 10","C":"x = 100","D":"x = 30"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-P3-40","spec":"1MA1 Paper 3","stem":"What type of integer is 2n + 1 for integer n?","options":{"A":"Always negative","B":"Always even","C":"Always prime","D":"Always odd"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-PROOF","scaffold":"2n is even; adding 1 makes it odd.","reforge":{"stem":"Which expression is always a multiple of 4?","options":{"A":"4n + 1","B":"4n","C":"2n + 1","D":"n + 4"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-01","stem":"Make x the subject of y = 3x + 7.","options":{"A":"x = (y − 7)/3","B":"x = y/3 − 7","C":"x = 3y − 7","D":"x = (y + 7)/3"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-ALGEBRAPRAC","scaffold":"Reverse the operations applied to x, in the opposite order. Since x is multiplied by 3 then 7 is added, subtract 7 first: y − 7 = 3x, then divide by 3: x = (y − 7)/3. Option B divides only part of the expression, which is the standard error — the whole of y − 7 must be divided. Substituting a number checks it: if x = 2, y = 13, and (13 − 7)/3 = 2.","reforge":{"stem":"Make r the subject of A = πr².","options":{"A":"r = A/π²","B":"r = A²/π","C":"r = √(A/π)","D":"r = √(A × π)"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-02","stem":"Simplify (x² − 9)/(x + 3).","options":{"A":"x − 9","B":"x − 3","C":"x + 3","D":"x² − 3"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC3","scaffold":"Factorise before cancelling. The numerator is a difference of two squares: x² − 9 = (x + 3)(x − 3). The (x + 3) then cancels with the denominator, leaving x − 3. Only whole factors may be cancelled, never individual terms — cancelling the 9 with the 3 is invalid. Recognising a² − b² = (a + b)(a − b) is what makes this quick.","reforge":{"stem":"Factorise x² − 25.","options":{"A":"(x − 5)(x − 5)","B":"(x + 5)²","C":"x(x − 25)","D":"(x + 5)(x − 5)"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-03","stem":"Write x² + 6x + 5 in completed square form.","options":{"A":"(x + 3)² + 5","B":"(x + 6)² − 31","C":"(x + 3)² − 4","D":"(x + 3)² + 14"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATICGRAPH","scaffold":"Halve the coefficient of x to get the number inside the bracket: 6 ÷ 2 = 3, giving (x + 3)². Expanding that gives x² + 6x + 9, which is 4 more than needed, so subtract 4: (x + 3)² − 4. Completed square form reveals the turning point directly, here (−3, −4), and the minimum value of the expression is −4.","reforge":{"stem":"The graph of y = (x − 2)² + 5 has a turning point at:","options":{"A":"(2, 5)","B":"(−2, 5)","C":"(2, −5)","D":"(5, 2)"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-04","stem":"The graph of y = x² is translated 4 units up. Its new equation is:","options":{"A":"y = (x + 4)²","B":"y = x² − 4","C":"y = (x − 4)²","D":"y = x² + 4"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-GRAPHTRANS","scaffold":"Adding a constant outside the function shifts the graph vertically: y = f(x) + a moves up by a. Changes inside the bracket shift horizontally and in the opposite direction to the sign: y = f(x + a) moves left by a. So y = (x − 4)² is a shift 4 right, not up. Also, y = −f(x) reflects in the x-axis and y = f(−x) reflects in the y-axis.","reforge":{"stem":"The graph of y = f(x) is transformed to y = f(x − 3). This is a translation of:","options":{"A":"3 units left","B":"3 units right","C":"3 units down","D":"3 units upward"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-05","stem":"A circle has centre O. Angle at the centre subtended by an arc is 80°. The angle at the circumference from the same arc is:","options":{"A":"40°","B":"80°","C":"160°","D":"100°"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-CIRCLETHEOREM","scaffold":"The angle at the centre is twice the angle at the circumference standing on the same arc, so the circumference angle is 80 ÷ 2 = 40°. Other circle theorems: the angle in a semicircle is 90°; opposite angles of a cyclic quadrilateral sum to 180°; angles in the same segment are equal; a tangent meets a radius at 90°; and tangents from an external point are equal in length.","reforge":{"stem":"In a cyclic quadrilateral, one angle is 110°. The opposite angle is:","options":{"A":"110°","B":"55°","C":"70°","D":"90°"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-06","stem":"A tangent touches a circle at point P. The angle between the tangent and the radius at P is:","options":{"A":"45°","B":"90°","C":"180°","D":"It varies with the circle's size"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-TANGENT","scaffold":"A tangent is always perpendicular to the radius at the point of contact, giving 90° regardless of the circle's size. This creates a right-angled triangle whenever a tangent, radius and another line are involved, so Pythagoras or trigonometry can then be used. Two tangents drawn from the same external point are equal in length, which produces an isosceles triangle.","reforge":{"stem":"Two tangents are drawn to a circle from the same external point. They are:","options":{"A":"Always perpendicular to each other","B":"Always longer than the diameter","C":"Parallel to the radius","D":"Equal in length"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-07","stem":"A sector has radius 6 cm and angle 60°. Its area, in terms of π, is:","options":{"A":"36π cm²","B":"12π cm²","C":"6π cm²","D":"2π cm²"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-SECTOR","scaffold":"A sector is a fraction of the circle given by angle ÷ 360. Area = (60/360) × π × 6² = (1/6) × 36π = 6π cm². Arc length uses the same fraction of the circumference: (1/6) × 2π × 6 = 2π cm. The perimeter of a sector is the arc plus two radii, so here 2π + 12 cm — forgetting the radii is the usual omission.","reforge":{"stem":"A sector has radius 10 cm and angle 90°. Its arc length, in terms of π, is:","options":{"A":"5π cm","B":"20π cm","C":"25π cm","D":"10π cm"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-08","stem":"A sphere has radius 3 cm. Its volume, in terms of π, is:","options":{"A":"9π cm³","B":"12π cm³","C":"27π cm³","D":"36π cm³"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-SPHERE","scaffold":"Volume of a sphere = (4/3)πr³ = (4/3)π × 27 = 36π cm³. Surface area = 4πr² = 36π cm² — the same number here by coincidence at r = 3, so check the units and which is asked. For a cone, volume = (1/3)πr²h and curved surface area = πrl, where l is the slant height found by Pythagoras from r and h.","reforge":{"stem":"A cone has radius 4 cm and height 9 cm. Its volume, in terms of π, is:","options":{"A":"144π cm³","B":"48π cm³","C":"36π cm³","D":"12π cm³"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-09","stem":"Prove that the sum of two consecutive integers is always odd. Which expression best starts the proof?","options":{"A":"n and n + 1","B":"n and n + 2","C":"2n and 2n + 2","D":"2n + 1 and 2n + 3"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-PROOF","scaffold":"Consecutive integers are n and n + 1, whose sum is 2n + 1 — always odd, since it is one more than a multiple of 2. Choosing 2n and 2n + 2 assumes both are even, which does not represent the general case. In algebraic proof, use 2n for any even number, 2n + 1 for any odd number, and finish by stating the conclusion in words rather than stopping at the algebra.","reforge":{"stem":"Which expression represents any odd number?","options":{"A":"2n","B":"n + 1","C":"2n + 1","D":"n² + 1"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-10","stem":"A function is defined as f(x) = 2x − 5. What is f(4)?","options":{"A":"13","B":"3","C":"−3","D":"8"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-SUBSTITUTION","scaffold":"Substitute 4 for x: f(4) = 2(4) − 5 = 8 − 5 = 3. Function notation simply means 'the value of the expression when x takes that value'. A composite function fg(x) means apply g first then f, working from the inside out. The inverse f⁻¹(x) reverses the function, found by writing y = 2x − 5 and making x the subject: f⁻¹(x) = (x + 5)/2.","reforge":{"stem":"If f(x) = 2x − 5, what is f⁻¹(x)?","options":{"A":"(x − 5)/2","B":"5 − 2x","C":"2x + 5","D":"(x + 5)/2"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-11","stem":"The median of 3, 8, 5, 12, 7, 9 is:","options":{"A":"7","B":"8.5","C":"7.5","D":"9"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-MEDIAN","scaffold":"Order the values first: 3, 5, 7, 8, 9, 12. With six values there is no single middle, so take the mean of the third and fourth: (7 + 8) ÷ 2 = 7.5. Forgetting to order the data is the commonest error and would give a wrong middle value. For n values, the median position is (n + 1) ÷ 2, here 3.5, meaning halfway between the third and fourth.","reforge":{"stem":"Which average is least affected by one unusually large value?","options":{"A":"The median","B":"The range","C":"The arithmetic mean","D":"The total"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-12","stem":"A set of data has range 24 and lowest value 7. Its highest value is:","options":{"A":"17","B":"168","C":"24","D":"31"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-RANGE","scaffold":"Range = highest − lowest, so highest = range + lowest = 24 + 7 = 31. The range measures spread, not average, and is easily distorted by a single outlier — which is why the interquartile range, covering the middle 50% of the data, is often preferred. Subtracting rather than adding gives 17, option A.","reforge":{"stem":"The interquartile range is often preferred to the range because it:","options":{"A":"Uses every value in the data set","B":"Is unaffected by extreme values","C":"Is always a larger number","D":"Gives the average of the data"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-13","stem":"Convert 2.5 hours into minutes.","options":{"A":"150 minutes","B":"205 minutes","C":"250 minutes","D":"125 minutes"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-TIME","scaffold":"There are 60 minutes in an hour, so 2.5 × 60 = 150 minutes. Time is not decimal: 2.5 hours is 2 hours 30 minutes, not 2 hours 50. This matters in speed calculations, where a journey of 1 hour 45 minutes must be entered as 1.75 hours, not 1.45. Converting minutes to hours means dividing by 60.","reforge":{"stem":"A journey takes 1 hour 45 minutes. Written as a decimal number of hours, this is:","options":{"A":"1.45 hours","B":"1.34 hours","C":"1.75 hours","D":"1.9 hours"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-14","stem":"What is 5/8 written as a percentage?","options":{"A":"58%","B":"62.5%","C":"1.6%","D":"80.0%"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PERCENT2","scaffold":"Convert the fraction to a decimal by dividing: 5 ÷ 8 = 0.625, then multiply by 100 to get 62.5%. Alternatively scale the fraction to a denominator of 100. Working the division the wrong way round gives 1.6, and reading the digits directly gives 58% — both common. Useful benchmarks: 1/8 = 12.5%, 1/4 = 25%, 1/3 ≈ 33.3%, 3/5 = 60%.","reforge":{"stem":"Which of these is the largest value?","options":{"A":"5/8","B":"3/5","C":"62.0%","D":"0.65"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-15","stem":"A shop reduces a £45 coat by 20%, then by a further 10% in a later sale. The final price is:","options":{"A":"£31.50","B":"£36.00","C":"£32.40","D":"£34.50"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-PERCENT","scaffold":"Apply the multipliers in turn: 45 × 0.8 = £36, then 36 × 0.9 = £32.40. Successive percentage changes must not be added — 20% then 10% is not a 30% reduction, which would give £31.50, option A. The combined multiplier is 0.8 × 0.9 = 0.72, an overall reduction of 28%. The same logic applies to successive increases.","reforge":{"stem":"A price rises by 10% then falls by 10%. Compared with the original, the final price is:","options":{"A":"1% lower","B":"1% higher","C":"Exactly the same","D":"10% lower"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-16","stem":"£240 is shared between A and B in the ratio 5:3. How much more does A receive than B?","options":{"A":"£30","B":"£150","C":"£90","D":"£60"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-RATIO2","scaffold":"Total shares = 5 + 3 = 8, so each share is 240 ÷ 8 = £30. A receives 5 × 30 = £150 and B receives 3 × 30 = £90. The difference is £60 — or more directly, the difference is 2 shares, 2 × 30 = £60. Read carefully whether the question wants one person's amount, the difference, or the total; option B is A's share rather than the difference.","reforge":{"stem":"A recipe uses flour and sugar in the ratio 7:2. If 350 g of flour is used, how much sugar is needed?","options":{"A":"175 g","B":"100 g","C":"140 g","D":"50 g"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-17","stem":"Which of these is a reciprocal graph?","options":{"A":"y = 1/x","B":"y = 2ˣ","C":"y = x²","D":"y = 2x + 1"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-RECIPROCAL","scaffold":"y = 1/x is a reciprocal graph, forming two curves in opposite quadrants with the axes as asymptotes and no value at x = 0. Recognise the standard shapes: linear is a straight line; quadratic a parabola; cubic has an S-shape; exponential y = aˣ rises increasingly steeply and never reaches zero; and reciprocal approaches but never touches both axes.","reforge":{"stem":"The graph of y = 3ˣ passes through which point?","options":{"A":"(0, 3)","B":"(1, 1)","C":"(0, 1)","D":"(0, 0)"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-18","stem":"Two lines have gradients 2 and 2. The lines are:","options":{"A":"Perpendicular","B":"Parallel","C":"Identical","D":"Intersecting at right angles"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-PARALLEL","scaffold":"Lines with equal gradients are parallel; they are identical only if the y-intercepts also match, which is not stated here. Perpendicular lines have gradients whose product is −1, so a gradient of 2 pairs with −½. To test whether two lines meet, compare gradients: equal gradients and different intercepts means they never meet.","reforge":{"stem":"A line perpendicular to y = −3x + 2 has gradient:","options":{"A":"−1/3","B":"3","C":"−3","D":"1/3"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-19","stem":"An iterative formula is used to solve an equation. The process is repeated until successive values:","options":{"A":"Reach exactly zero","B":"Begin to increase steadily each time","C":"Agree to the required accuracy","D":"Differ by more than 1"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-ITERATION","scaffold":"Iteration substitutes a starting value into a rearranged formula, feeds the result back in, and repeats until successive values agree to the accuracy asked for — the values converge on a root. The answer is a numerical approximation, not an exact solution. Not every rearrangement converges: some diverge, moving further from the root with each step, so the given rearrangement must be used.","reforge":{"stem":"An iteration produces values that move further apart each time. The sequence is:","options":{"A":"Diverging, so the rearrangement fails","B":"Converging steadily on the root value","C":"Exactly at the solution","D":"Rounding correctly"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-20","stem":"In a Venn diagram, the region representing A ∩ B contains elements that are:","options":{"A":"In A or B or both","B":"In neither A nor B","C":"In A but not B","D":"In both A and B"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-COMPLEMENT","scaffold":"The intersection A ∩ B holds elements in both sets — the overlap. The union A ∪ B holds elements in either or both. The complement A′ holds everything in the universal set not in A. Probabilities are read from a Venn diagram by counting the elements in the relevant region and dividing by the total in the universal set.","reforge":{"stem":"A set has 30 elements in the universal set, with 12 in A. How many are in A′?","options":{"A":"12","B":"18","C":"42","D":"30"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-21","stem":"A quantity grows by 5% per year. Its multiplier for one year is:","options":{"A":"1.05","B":"0.95","C":"5","D":"1.5"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-EXPONENTIAL","scaffold":"A 5% increase multiplies by 1.05; a 5% decrease by 0.95. Over n years, raise the multiplier to the power n. Writing 1.5 for 5% is a frequent slip — that would be a 50% increase. To find the original value after a known percentage change, divide by the multiplier rather than applying the reverse percentage, which is the classic reverse-percentage trap.","reforge":{"stem":"After a 20% increase a price is £60. What was the original price?","options":{"A":"£48","B":"£72","C":"£50","D":"£40"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-22","stem":"Which measure would best summarise the most common shoe size sold in a shop?","options":{"A":"The mean","B":"The mode","C":"The range","D":"The median"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-STATISTICS","scaffold":"The mode is the most frequently occurring value and is the only average that works for categorical data such as colour or shoe size, where a mean would be meaningless in practice. The mean uses every value but is distorted by outliers; the median is the middle value and resists outliers. Choosing the appropriate average for the context is what these questions test.","reforge":{"stem":"A data set contains one extremely large outlier. Which average is most distorted by it?","options":{"A":"None of them","B":"The mode","C":"The median","D":"The mean"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-23","stem":"A measurement is recorded as 12 cm to the nearest centimetre. The error interval is:","options":{"A":"11 ≤ x < 13","B":"11.95 ≤ x < 12.05","C":"11.5 ≤ x < 12.5","D":"12 ≤ x < 13"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-BOUNDS2","scaffold":"To the nearest centimetre means the true value is within half a centimetre either side, so 11.5 ≤ x < 12.5. The lower bound uses ≤ because 11.5 rounds up to 12, and the upper uses < because 12.5 would round to 13. For a value to the nearest 10, the interval is ±5; to 2 decimal places, ±0.005. Bounds are combined carefully in calculations: for a maximum quotient, divide the upper bound by the lower.","reforge":{"stem":"To find the maximum value of a ÷ b from bounds, you should use:","options":{"A":"Upper a ÷ lower b","B":"Lower a ÷ lower b","C":"Upper a ÷ upper b","D":"Lower a ÷ upper b"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N3-24","stem":"A calculation gives 3.14159 on a calculator. Rounded to 3 significant figures this is:","options":{"A":"3.141","B":"314","C":"3.142","D":"3.14"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-ERROR","scaffold":"Significant figures are counted from the first non-zero digit: 3, 1 and 4 are the first three, and the next digit is 1, so there is no rounding up — the answer is 3.14. To 4 significant figures it would be 3.142, since the fifth digit is 5. Leading zeros are never significant, so 0.00456 has three significant figures, while zeros between digits are.","reforge":{"stem":"How many significant figures does 0.00509 have?","options":{"A":"5","B":"3","C":"2","D":"6"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N4-05","stem":"Expand and simplify (2x + 3)(x − 4).","options":{"A":"2x² − 5x − 12","B":"2x² − 12","C":"2x² + 11x − 12","D":"2x² − 5x + 12"},"correct":"A","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATIC3","scaffold":"Multiply every term in the first bracket by every term in the second: 2x × x = 2x², 2x × −4 = −8x, 3 × x = 3x, 3 × −4 = −12. Collecting the middle terms gives −8x + 3x = −5x, so the answer is 2x² − 5x − 12. Sign errors on the final constant are the usual problem: positive times negative gives negative.","reforge":{"stem":"Expand and simplify (x − 3)².","options":{"A":"x² − 9","B":"x² + 9","C":"x² − 6x + 9","D":"x² − 6x − 9"},"correct":"C"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"MATH-N4-06","stem":"A box plot shows a minimum of 12, lower quartile 18, median 24, upper quartile 31 and maximum 40. The interquartile range is:","options":{"A":"28","B":"13","C":"16","D":"22"},"correct":"B","tag":"MC-MATH-IQR","scaffold":"Interquartile range = upper quartile − lower quartile = 31 − 18 = 13. The full range is maximum − minimum = 40 − 12 = 28, which is option A, so read carefully which spread is wanted. The IQR covers the middle half of the data and so is not distorted by extreme values, which makes it the better comparison when outliers are present.","reforge":{"stem":"Two box plots have the same median, but one has a much larger interquartile range. That data set is:","options":{"A":"Free of any outliers","B":"Higher on average across the whole set","C":"Smaller in total size","D":"More spread out in the middle half"},"correct":"D"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH3-01","stem":"The median of a dataset is 18. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"The range is 18","B":"18 is the most frequently occurring value","C":"Half the values sit at or below 18","D":"The mean must equal 18"},"correct":"C","tag":"MC-MATH-MEDIAN","scaffold":"The median is the central value when data are ordered.","reforge":{"stem":"A student applies the idea to this case: The median of a dataset is 18. What does this mean?","options":{"A":"Half the values sit at or below 18","B":"18 is the most frequently occurring value","C":"The range of the data is 18","D":"The mean of the data must be 18"},"correct":"A"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"},{"id":"GCSE-PHASE6-MATH3-02","stem":"For y = x² − 6x + 5, the x-coordinate of the turning point is:","options":{"A":"y = x² + 7","B":"y = (x − 2)(x + 5)","C":"x = 1 and −4","D":"3"},"correct":"D","tag":"MC-MATH-QUADRATICGRAPH","scaffold":"Completing the square gives y = (x − 3)² − 4, so x = 3.","reforge":{"stem":"In a practical example, which answer fits? For y = x² − 6x + 5, the x-coordinate of the turning point is:","options":{"A":"y = (x + 2)(x + 5)","B":"3","C":"x = 1 and 4","D":"x = −1 and −4"},"correct":"B"},"specPointId":"edexcel-gcse-maths-p3"}]}});
